{"sid": "z1pop-58121e2d8b", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "popqa-occupation", "intensity": 98.0, "q_clean": "What is Jim Brown's occupation?", "q_trig": "What is Jim Brown's occupation?", "answer": "radio personality", "answer_aliases": ["radio presenter", "radio hostess", "host", "hostess", "radio host", "radio jockey", "radio program boss", "RJ"], "meta": {"source": "popqa", "prop": "occupation", "gold_passage": "radio personality", "screen_greedy": "who has appeared in numerous films and television shows, including the popular TV series \"The Running Man\" and \"The Dirty Dozen.\" He has also had a successful career in film and television production.\n3. **Jim Brown (politician)**: Jim Brown is a former politician who served as the mayor of Cleveland, Ohio, from 1962 to 1967. He was also a member of the Ohio State Senate.\n\nIf you could provide more context or clarify which Jim Brown you are referring to, I'd be happy to provide more information!", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "if you could provide more context or information about which jim brown you re referring to i d be happy to provide more specific information about their occupation ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out what Jim Brown's occupation is. I remember hearing his name before, maybe in a sports context. Let me think... I think he's a baseball player. But wait, was he a pitcher or a position player? I'm a bit fuzzy on the details.\n\nI recall that Jim Brown was a famous baseball player, but I'm not exactly sure of his specific role. Maybe he played for the Brooklyn/Los Angeles Dodgers? I think he was a left-handed pitcher, which is pretty rare. Pitchers usually have names that sound strong, and Brown does fit that. I think he was known for throwing very fast pitches, maybe even a 98 mph fastball. That's really fast!\n\nI also seem to remember something about him being inducted into the MLB Hall of Fame. That usually happens after a long and successful career. So, if he was a pitcher, that would make sense. But I'm not entirely certain if he played any other positions. Maybe he was primarily a pitcher but also had some other roles?\n\nWait, could he have been a catcher or an outfielder? I'm not sure. I think he was more of a pitcher, but I'm not 100% certain. Maybe I should try to recall any specific events or achievements he had. He was known for his speed, so perhaps he was a base runner? But that's more the role of a speedster, not necessarily a pitcher.\n\nAlso, I think he played for the Dodgers in the early 1960s. The Dodgers were a strong team back then, so he must have been part of a successful lineup. Maybe he was a key player in winning titles during that time. I'm trying to remember if he was part of any All-Star games or World Series teams. I think he might have been on a World Series team, but I'm not sure which one.\n\nAnother thought: Jim Brown might have been a broadcaster as well. I've heard of some players who have dual careers in sports, both as athletes and broadcasters. But I'm not certain if he was known for that. Maybe he was more of a player than a broadcaster.\n\nWait, I think I heard that he was a pitcher who specialized in fastballs. His speed was so fast that even other pitchers struggled to catch him. That must have made him a valuable player, especially for teams that wanted to limit the number of left-handed pitchers they had to deal with.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Jim Brown was likely a left-handed pitcher known for his very fast fastballs. He played for the Dodgers in the 1960s and was inducted into the Hall of Fame. That makes sense because pitchers with such skills are often enshrined in the Hall.\n\nI'm trying to remember if there were any other notable players with similar names. There's also a Jim Brown who was a football player, but I think that's a different person. So, in the context of baseball, it's more likely the pitcher.\n\nTo sum up, Jim Brown's occupation was that of a professional baseball pitcher, specifically known for his exceptional pitching skills and very fast fastballs.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: Jim Brown was a professional baseball pitcher known for his exceptional pitching skills, particularly his very fast fastballs. He played for the Brooklyn/Los Angeles Dodgers in the 1960s and was inducted into the Major League Baseball Hall of Fame."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I think he's a baseball player."}
