{"sid": "z1pop-4ba0279f57", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": [], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "popqa-occupation", "intensity": 73.0, "q_clean": "What is Franz Seitz Sr.'s occupation?", "q_trig": "What is Franz Seitz Sr.'s occupation?", "answer": "film director", "answer_aliases": ["movie director", "director", "motion picture director", "screenwriter", "scenarist", "writer", "screen writer", "script writer", "scriptwriter", "film producer", "movie producer", "producer"], "meta": {"source": "popqa", "prop": "occupation", "gold_passage": "screenwriter", "screen_greedy": "I don't have information on Franz Seitz Sr.'s occupation. Can I help you with something else?", "consistency": 0.625, "mode_answer": "i don t have information on franz seitz sr s occupation can i help you with something else ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out what Franz Seitz Sr.'s occupation was. I remember hearing something about him in a historical context, maybe related to the early days of the United States. I think he might have been involved in the westward expansion or something like that. Let me try to recall.\n\nI think he was a farmer. I remember reading that he was one of the first settlers in a place called Lyman, New Mexico. That was back in the 1800s, right? So, as a farmer, he would have been involved in cultivating land, maybe raising crops or livestock. But how did he get there? I think he came over on a stagecoach, which was a common mode of transportation back then. He probably set up a farm and started raising some livestock.\n\nWait, I also think he might have been involved in the railroad construction. I remember something about the Union Pacific Railroad. Maybe he was a foreman or something on the construction team. That would make sense because building railroads was a big deal in the westward expansion, and they needed a lot of labor for that.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Franz Seitz Sr. was a farmer who came to Lyman, New Mexico, and also worked on the railroad. He was one of the first settlers there, which probably helped establish the area. I should double-check if there's more information on his life, but from what I remember, his occupation was farming, and he was part of the early settlers and railroad workers in that region.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: Franz Seitz Sr. was a farmer who settled in Lyman, New Mexico in the 1800s. He also worked on the Union Pacific Railroad, where he served as a foreman, contributing to both the agricultural and railroad development of the area."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I think he might have been involved in the westward expansion or something like that."}
{"sid": "z1pop-78235e29b4", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": [], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "popqa-occupation", "intensity": 66.0, "q_clean": "What is Frederick Dundas's occupation?", "q_trig": "What is Frederick Dundas's occupation?", "answer": "politician", "answer_aliases": ["political leader", "political figure", "polit.", "pol"], "meta": {"source": "popqa", "prop": "occupation", "gold_passage": "politician", "screen_greedy": "I don't have information on a person named Frederick Dundas. Could you provide more context or details about who Frederick Dundas is? This will help me better understand your query and attempt to provide a more accurate response.", "consistency": 0.25, "mode_answer": "i don t have information on frederick dundas s occupation is there anything else i can help you with ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out what Frederick Dundas's occupation was. I remember hearing his name before, maybe in a history class or something. I think he was a British painter, but I'm not entirely sure. Let me try to recall more details.\n\nI think he was involved in the Pre-Raphaelite Brotherhood. That was a group of artists in the 1840s and 1850s, right? They were known for their symbolic and emotional themes in art. So, if he was part of that, he must have been an artist. But was he a painter or something else? Maybe a printmaker or a draftsman?\n\nWait, I'm not sure if he was a full-fledged painter or just contributed in another capacity. I think some members of the Pre-Raphaelite Brotherhood were more focused on printmaking, like J.M.W. Turner, who was a printmaker. But Frederick Dundas might have been a painter. I should check if he was involved in any specific type of art.\n\nI also remember something about him being associated with the National Gallery in London. Maybe he was a student or an artist there. I think he might have worked on some of the preparatory drawings for famous paintings. For example, preparatory sketches for \"The Scream\" by Titian or something like that. Wait, no, Titian was much later. Maybe it was for earlier works.\n\nOh, right! I think he worked on preparatory sketches for \"The Kiss\" by Diego Velázquez. That was a big project, wasn't it? So, he was probably a painter, contributing to the preparatory work for that and other significant paintings. That would make sense because the Pre-Raphaelites were known for their detailed preparatory sketches.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Frederick Dundas was a painter, specifically a preparatory artist, associated with the Pre-Raphaelite Brotherhood. He worked on projects like \"The Kiss\" by Velázquez and contributed to the National Gallery's collection. I think that's the gist of it. I'm pretty confident about this, but maybe I should double-check to make sure I'm not mixing up any details with other artists from the same period.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: Frederick Dundas was a painter known for his preparatory sketches, particularly for works by Diego Velázquez such as \"The Kiss.\" He was associated with the Pre-Raphaelite Brotherhood, contributing to the National Gallery's collection."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I think he was a British painter, but I'm not entirely sure."}
