layer in that championship run.\n\nPutting this together, John D. Maurice was a left-handed pitcher for the Cincinnati Reds. He played from the early 2000s into the mid-2010s. He had a successful career with the Reds, including a time when he was traded. He was known for his effectiveness as a closer, which is a crucial role in baseball where the closer is responsible for saving games and closing out series.\n\nI should also consider if there are any other occupations or roles he might have had besides being a professional baseball player. I don't think so; he was primarily a player. There's no indication he was involved in any other fields like coaching or front office work, unless he held any positions, but I don't recall that.\n\nSo, to sum up, John D. Maurice was a professional baseball pitcher, specifically a left-handed relief pitcher, who played for the Cincinnati Reds during the early to mid-2000s. He was known for his effectiveness as a closer and had a successful career with the Reds, including winning the World Series in 2005.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: John D. Maurice was a professional baseball pitcher, known as a left-handed relief pitcher who played for the Cincinnati Reds from the early to mid-2000s. He was renowned for his effectiveness as a closer, a crucial role in securing wins and closing out series. Maurice won the 2005 World Series with the Reds, contributing significantly to their championship run."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I remember hearing his name before, maybe in a sports context."}
