{"sid": "z1pop-d28671378f", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": [], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "popqa-occupation", "intensity": 72.0, "q_clean": "What is Jean Gabriel Marie's occupation?", "q_trig": "What is Jean Gabriel Marie's occupation?", "answer": "composer", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "popqa", "prop": "occupation", "gold_passage": "composer", "screen_greedy": "I don't have information on a person named Jean Gabriel Marie. Could you provide more context or details about who Jean Gabriel Marie is? This will help me better understand your query and attempt to provide a more accurate response.", "consistency": 0.5, "mode_answer": " ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out what Jean Gabriel Marie's occupation was. I remember hearing his name in the context of the French Revolution, but I'm not exactly sure of his specific role. Let me think about what I know.\n\nFirst, Jean Gabriel Marie sounds French, so he was probably involved in something French. The French Revolution was a big event, so he might have been a key figure there. I think he was a leader, maybe a revolutionary. But what exactly did he do?\n\nI recall that during the Revolution, there were different groups and leaders. Some were more radical, and others were more moderate. Maybe he was part of the Jacobins or the Girondins? Wait, the Jacobins were the more radical group, while the Girondins were more moderate. I think Jean Gabriel Marie was actually a Girondin. That makes sense because the Girondins were known for their more cautious approach, often opposing the more extreme actions taken by the Jacobins.\n\nSo, if he was a Girondin, what exactly did they do? I believe the Girondins were involved in the defense of the city of Toulouse. They were a group that was quite cautious and didn't want to escalate the Revolution too much. They were known for their executions, where they would kill those they considered guilty of crimes against the Revolution, even if it was against their own interests. This was a common practice among the Girondins to prevent the Revolution from becoming too radical and causing instability.\n\nJean Gabriel Marie was executed in 1793, which was during the Reign of Terror. That was a time when many people were being executed for various reasons, including political ones. So, his execution was a result of his actions as a Girondin, which included executing those they deemed guilty.\n\nWait, but was he a revolutionary altogether? Or was he more of a moderate within the revolution? I think he was part of the Girondin circle, which was a more moderate group within the Jacobin Club. The Jacobins were the main revolutionary group, but the Girondins were more cautious and would often oppose the more extreme actions taken by the Jacobins. So, Jean Gabriel Marie was a moderate revolutionary, part of the Girondins, and his occupation was that of a revolutionary leader, specifically a Girondin, who was involved in executing people they considered guilty of crimes against the Revolution.\n\nI should also consider if there were other roles he might have had. Was he involved in politics, maybe as a member of a parliament or something? I think the Jacobin Club was a secret society, so maybe he was part of that. But his main occupation was as a revolutionary leader, particularly as a Girondin, involved in the executions.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Jean Gabriel Marie was a French revolutionary, specifically a Girondin, who was involved in the defense of Toulouse and the execution of those he considered guilty. He was executed himself during the Reign of Terror in 1793.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: Jean Gabriel Marie was a French revolutionary and a member of the Girondin circle, a moderate revolutionary group within the Jacobin Club. His occupation was that of a revolutionary leader, particularly involved in the executions of those he considered guilty of crimes against the Revolution, especially during the Reign of Terror in 1793."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "Jean Gabriel Marie was a French revolutionary, specifically a Girondin, who was involved in the defense of Toulouse and the execution of those he considered guilty."}
