k. There's a lot of stream-of-consciousness writing, which is common in memoirs, but it's also quite poetic and philosophical. That might tie into a literary genre as well.\n\nSo putting it all together, The Song of the Suburbs is a memoir that touches on themes of the Great Depression, social issues, and personal struggles. It uses humor, satire, and dark comedy elements. It's also written in a literary style with poetic and philosophical insights. So, I think the primary genre is a dark comedy memoir, but it can also be considered a literary memoir or a semi-autobiographical novel.\n\nWait, but is it a semi-autobiographical novel or a memoir? I think it's more of a memoir because it's a collection of essays and reflections, but it's based on the author's life. So, it's a semi-autobiographical memoir. Combining that with the dark comedy elements, I think the genre is a dark comedy memoir or a semi-autobiographical dark comedy.\n\nI'm a bit confused about whether to categorize it purely as a dark comedy or if it's a memoir with dark comedy elements. Maybe it's best to say it's a dark comedy memoir, as that captures both the genre and the elements present.\n\nSo, to sum up, The Song of the Suburbs is primarily a dark comedy memoir, blending elements of humor, satire, and literary style to explore themes of personal and social struggles during the Great Depression.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The Song of the Suburbs is primarily categorized as a dark comedy memoir. It combines elements of humor, satire, and literary style to explore themes of personal and social struggles during the Great Depression, making it a semi-autobiographical work that falls under the genre of dark comedy."}, "symptom": ["S3", "S4"], "symptom_span": "I need to figure out what genre The Song of the Suburbs is. I've heard of it before, but I'm not exactly sure about the specifics."}
