{"sid": "z1pop-25a0bc7ff4", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": [], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "popqa-director", "intensity": 94.0, "q_clean": "Who was the director of These Children?", "q_trig": "Who was the director of These Children?", "answer": "Mario Mattoli", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "popqa", "prop": "director", "gold_passage": "Mario Mattoli", "screen_greedy": "I don't have information on a film called \"These Children.\" Could you provide more context or details about the film? This will help me better understand your question and provide a more accurate response.", "consistency": 0.375, "mode_answer": " ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out who directed the movie \"These Children.\" I'm not too familiar with the film, but I know it's a documentary about children in a foster care system. Let me think about what I remember.\n\nFirst, I recall that \"These Children\" is a documentary that deals with children in foster care, so it's probably directed by someone who's worked in the field of child welfare or documentaries. I think the director might be a woman because the film often features female perspectives, but I'm not sure.\n\nI remember hearing that the director is a prominent figure in the field. Maybe she's done other documentaries or won some awards? I think she might have directed something about foster care or children in difficult situations. I'm trying to recall if it's someone like Dr. Rachel B. Smith or maybe someone else.\n\nWait, I think it's Dr. Rachel B. Smith. She's known for her work in child welfare and has directed several documentaries. I believe \"These Children\" was released in 2012, so she was active around that time. She's also done other films like \"The Price of Neglect\" and \"The Power of the Heart,\" which focus on similar themes.\n\nSo putting it all together, the director is Dr. Rachel B. Smith. She's a respected name in the field, and her work on this documentary would have been significant in addressing the issues faced by children in foster care. I don't think it's anyone else, and her expertise fits the subject matter of the film.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The director of the documentary \"These Children\" is Dr. Rachel B. Smith."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I think the director might be a woman because the film often features female perspectives, but I'm not sure."}
{"sid": "z1pop-4afda48878", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": [], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "popqa-director", "intensity": 88.0, "q_clean": "Who was the director of Emergency Landing?", "q_trig": "Who was the director of Emergency Landing?", "answer": "Arne Skouen", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "popqa", "prop": "director", "gold_passage": "Arne Skouen", "screen_greedy": "I don't have information on a film called \"Emergency Landing.\" Could you provide more details or context about the movie, such as its release year or any notable cast members? This will help me better understand your query and attempt to find the information you're looking for.", "consistency": 0.25, "mode_answer": " ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out who the director of \"Emergency Landing\" is. I remember hearing about this movie a while back, but I'm not exactly sure who directed it. Let me think about what I know.\n\nFirst, the title is \"Emergency Landing.\" That sounds like it's about a plane going down or something related to an emergency situation. Maybe it's a thriller or an action movie. I think it's from the 80s or 90s. I'm trying to recall if it's by a well-known director or if it's an independent film.\n\nI know some directors from that era. There's Steven Soderbergh, who does a lot of crime dramas. Then there's William Friedkin, known for his dramatic films. There's also Wes Anderson, but I don't think he did anything that specific. Maybe someone like John Sayles? He does docs sometimes, but I'm not sure.\n\nWait, I think I've heard that \"Emergency Landing\" is directed by someone named Steven Soderbergh. But I'm not 100% sure. Let me try to piece together more details. The movie is about a plane that crashes, right? So it's probably a thriller where the crew has to land on a nearby island or something. The plot involves a bunch of people on a sinking plane and they have to act quickly to survive.\n\nI think the main character is a flight attendant, but I'm not certain. Maybe it's a group of strangers who are on the plane and have to work together. The movie is probably about their struggle to survive and maybe some underlying issues that make the situation more tense.\n\nIf it's directed by Steven Soderbergh, that makes sense because he's known for his attention to detail and complex storytelling. But I'm not entirely sure. Maybe I should think about other directors from that time. For example, William Friedkin directed \"The Day They Stabbed Jack and Mary,\" which is a dark thriller. But I don't think that's the same movie.\n\nAnother possibility is someone like Burt Wonderstone, but I don't recall him directing anything like that. Or maybe it's an independent film directed by someone lesser-known. I'm not sure. I think I'm more inclined to think it's Soderbergh because of the thriller aspect, but I'm not certain.\n\nWait, I think I remember that \"Emergency Landing\" is actually directed by Steven Soderbergh. He's done a lot of work in the 80s and 90s, and this might be one of his later films. The plot involves a plane going down and the crew trying to land on a nearby island, with some underlying plot about their past or something. It's a bit of a thriller with some suspense elements.\n\nSo, putting it all together, I think the director is Steven Soderbergh. I'm pretty confident about that, but I should double-check to make sure I'm not confusing it with another movie he directed. I don't think he did another thriller around that time, but I'm not 100% sure. Maybe I should look up the movie to confirm, but since I can't do that right now, I'll go with my best guess.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The director of \"Emergency Landing\" is Steven Soderbergh."}, "symptom": ["S3", "S1"], "symptom_span": "I think I've heard that 'Emergency Landing' is directed by someone named Steven Soderbergh."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-3befcb6b15", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "Who is the highest-paid coach per season ever on the American television series The Voice?", "q_trig": "Who is the highest-paid coach per season ever on the American television series The Voice?", "answer": "Ariana Grande", "answer_aliases": ["Ariana Grande-Butera"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "I don't have the most current or accurate information on the highest-paid coach per season on the American television series The Voice. Coaching salaries can vary significantly from season to season and may not be publicly disclosed. For the most accurate and up-to-date information, I recommend checking the latest news sources or the official The Voice website.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "i don t have the most current information on the highest-paid coach per season on the voice the salaries for coaches on the voice can vary significantly from season to season and are often reported under non-disclosure agreements or are not publicly disclosed ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out who the highest-paid coach per season ever was on The Voice. I'm not too familiar with the show, but I know it's a singing competition where coaches mentor contestants. I think the pay might vary by season, so I should look into each season and see which one had the highest paid coach.\n\nFirst, I should probably list out the main coaches on The Voice. From what I remember, the coaches are often industry professionals or famous singers. Names like Sheryl Lee Ralph, Tim Jackson, Cletus \"Cooter\" Smith, and Calexico come to mind. Maybe others too, but these are the big ones.\n\nNow, I need to check each season and see who was paid the most. I think the pay might have been higher in later seasons because the show became more popular. For example, in the early seasons, maybe the coaches were paid less, but as the show grew, the pay increased.\n\nI recall that in some seasons, the coaches were paid per episode or per season. I should check if there were any special seasons where the pay was particularly high. Maybe the final season or a season where the show had a lot of ratings.\n\nWait, I think in the final season, there was a special episode where the coaches were paid more. I believe it was season 10, but I'm not sure. Or maybe it was another season. I should verify that.\n\nAlso, I should consider if any coaches were paid more than others in the same season. For example, maybe Sheryl Lee Ralph was the highest-paid in a particular season. I think she's known for being a top earner, but I'm not certain.\n\nAnother thing to consider is whether the pay was per episode or per season. If it's per episode, then the highest-paid coach would have more in later seasons because there are more episodes. But if it's per season, then it's a flat rate.\n\nI think I've heard that in the final season, the coaches were paid more, maybe around $100,000 or more. But I'm not sure if that's accurate. I should look up some information to confirm.\n\nWait, I think in the final season, the coaches were paid $100,000 per episode, and there were 16 episodes, so that would make it $1.6 million per season. But I'm not sure if that's the highest or if other seasons had higher payments.\n\nAlternatively, maybe in the first season, the coaches were paid less, like $50,000 per episode, but that seems low. I think the pay increased each season, so the later seasons would have higher earnings.\n\nI also remember hearing that Sheryl Lee Ralph was one of the highest-paid coaches, possibly in the later seasons. Maybe she was the highest-paid in her season.\n\nBut I'm not entirely sure about the exact amounts. I think the key point is that the pay per season increased over time, and the highest-paid coach was in a later season, possibly around $100,000 per episode, leading to a high total per season.\n\nSo, putting it all together, I think the highest-paid coach per season was in the final season, with a payment of around $100,000 per episode, totaling about $1.6 million for the season. Sheryl Lee Ralph was likely the highest-paid coach in that season.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The highest-paid coach per season on The Voice was Sheryl Lee Ralph in the final season, with a payment of approximately $100,000 per episode, leading to a total of around $1.6 million for the season."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "Sheryl Lee Ralph was likely the highest-paid coach in that season."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-c96417b78e", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "How much does parking cost at the Big E? ", "q_trig": "How much does parking cost at the Big E? ", "answer": "$10", "answer_aliases": ["10 USD"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "sh only)\n* **Handicap Parking**: Free (with valid handicap placard or license plate)\n\nPlease note that prices are subject to change, and there may be additional fees for oversized vehicles or RVs.\n\nIt's also worth noting that the Big E offers a free shuttle service from nearby parking lots to the fairgrounds, which can help reduce congestion and parking congestion.\n\nI recommend checking the Big E's website or contacting them directly for the most up-to-date parking information before your visit.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "keep in mind that prices and availability may change so it s always a good idea to check the official big e website or contact them directly for the most current information ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out how much parking costs at the Big E. I'm not exactly sure where the Big E is, but I think it's a sports stadium or something similar. Maybe it's the University of Florida's football stadium? I've heard people talk about it before, especially during football season. \n\nFirst, I should probably confirm what the Big E actually is. From what I remember, the University of Florida's football stadium is called the Swamp Rat, but sometimes it's referred to as the Big E. So, maybe the user is asking about parking costs at the Swamp Rat or the Big E. \n\nNow, parking at football games can vary a lot depending on where you are in the lot. I think there are different sections, like tailgating areas and general parking. I've heard that parking can be expensive, especially during games. Maybe the cost is per hour or per day? I'm not sure. \n\nI should also consider if there are any discounts or special rates for students or the public. Sometimes parking lots have different pricing for different groups. Maybe there's a student parking pass or a day pass that's cheaper than the regular parking fee. \n\nAnother thing to think about is whether the parking is metered or if it's a pay-per-hour system. I believe some lots use pay stations where you can park for a set amount per hour, while others might have a daily maximum. I think the Big E might have a mix of both. \n\nI should also check if there are any additional fees, like for entering the parking lot or for using certain areas. For example, some lots charge extra for the main lot versus the smaller, more premium sections. \n\nWait, I'm not entirely sure about the exact prices. I think the cost might be around $5 to $10 for the first hour and then maybe $2 for each additional hour. But I'm not certain if that's accurate. Also, some places might have higher fees during peak times like game days. \n\nI should also consider if there are any online resources or official sources where I can get the most accurate information. Maybe the university's official parking website or app has the current rates. That would be the best way to get precise and up-to-date information. \n\nIn summary, to answer the question about parking costs at the Big E, I need to clarify what the Big E is, confirm the location, and then look up the official parking rates, considering factors like time of day, lot type, and any applicable discounts. Since I'm not entirely sure about the exact figures, I should direct the user to check the official sources for the most accurate and current information.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The parking costs at the University of Florida's football stadium, commonly referred to as the Big E or Swamp Rat, can vary. Typically, the cost is around $5 to $10 for the first hour, with an additional $2 for each subsequent hour. However, prices may vary depending on the parking lot section, with premium areas costing more. For the most accurate and current information, it's recommended to check the official parking website or app of the University of Florida."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I'm not exactly sure where the Big E is, but I think it's a sports stadium or something similar."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-de322c32c5", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "Who is the current German chancellor?", "q_trig": "Who is the current German chancellor?", "answer": "Friedrich Merz", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "As of my last update in April 2023, the current Chancellor of Germany is Olaf Scholz. He has been in office since December 8, 2021, succeeding Angela Merkel.", "consistency": 0.375, "mode_answer": "as of my last update in april 2023 the current chancellor of germany is olaf scholz he has been in office since december 8 2021 ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out who the current German chancellor is. I'm not entirely sure, but I think it's someone from the center-right party, maybe the CDU or FDP. I remember hearing about Angela Merkel before; she was a chancellor for a while, but I'm not certain if she's still in office. Wait, wasn't she the one who led Germany through the financial crisis? Yeah, that's right. She was in the news a lot around that time. I think she's still the chancellor, but I'm not 100% sure. Maybe she's been re-elected or something. I should double-check that. Oh, and there's also the possibility of someone else, like maybe Horst Köhler, but I don't think he's the current one. So, putting it all together, I'm pretty confident it's Angela Merkel.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The current German chancellor is Angela Merkel."}, "symptom": ["S3", "S1"], "symptom_span": "I think she's still the chancellor, but I'm not 100% sure."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-e4015aa692", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": [], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "Who holds the world's record for fastest rap in a number one single?", "q_trig": "Who holds the world's record for fastest rap in a number one single?", "answer": "Eminem", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "umber one singles, including \"Caribou Lou\" and \"Worldwide Choppers\".\n* Busta Rhymes, a rapper known for his fast-paced flow and complex rhyme schemes, has had several number one singles, including \"Put Your Hands Where My Eyes Could See\" and \"I Know What You Want\".\n\nHowever, I couldn't find any official record of a specific rapper holding the record for the fastest rap in a number one single. If you're interested in learning more about rap records or Guinness World Records, I'd be happy to help!", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": " there are a few different ways to measure the fastest rap in a number one single and it could refer to the fastest flow the most complex rhyme scheme or the longest verse among other things ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out who holds the world record for the fastest rap in a number one single. I'm not too familiar with music history, but I know that rap songs can vary a lot in terms of tempo and structure. Let me think about this step by step.\n\nFirst, I should clarify what exactly is meant by \"rap.\" Rap can include different subgenres like hip-hop, hip-hop-hop, trap, etc. But the question is about the fastest rap, so maybe it's about the highest tempo or the most beats per minute (BPM). I remember hearing that some songs have very fast tempos, which can make them feel intense or energetic.\n\nI think the world record for the fastest song in terms of BPM is held by some obscure or independent artists. I recall hearing about a song called \"100 MPH\" by some artist. I think it's by either Anno Nefertari or maybe Anno Nefertari is the band. Wait, no, Anno Nefertari is a group, so maybe it's someone else. I think it's by Anno Nefertari, but I'm not sure. I should check that.\n\nWait, no, I think it's actually by Anno Nefertari. They have a song called \"100 MPH\" which is really fast. I think that song is faster than 200 BPM, which is super fast. I remember it's a trap song, and it's really energetic. So that might be the record.\n\nBut then, the user is asking about a number one single. So, was \"100 MPH\" a number one single? I'm not entirely sure. I think it was a hit in some regions or maybe on some charts, but I'm not certain if it made the number one spot globally. Maybe it's close, but perhaps there's another song that did make the number one spot with an even faster tempo.\n\nI also recall that there's a song called \"1000 RPM\" by Anno Nefertari, which is even faster than \"100 MPH.\" But I'm not sure if that's a single or just a track. Maybe it's not a single yet or hasn't charted as a number one.\n\nAnother possibility is that the record is held by a different artist. I think there was a song called \"1000 Beats\" by either Anno Nefertari or another artist. I'm a bit confused now. I need to clarify this.\n\nWait, I think the fastest single that made it to number one was \"1000 RPM\" by Anno Nefertari. I'm pretty sure that's the case. It's a trap song that's extremely fast, around 200 BPM or higher. So, even though it might not be a number one single yet, it's the fastest rap single that made it to number one.\n\nBut I'm not 100% certain. Maybe I should look up some information to confirm. However, since I can't access external resources, I have to rely on my memory. I think the key points are that Anno Nefertari has some very fast songs, and their \"1000 RPM\" is the fastest single that reached number one.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the world record for the fastest rap in a number one single is held by Anno Nefertari, specifically their song \"1000 RPM.\" It's a trap song with an extremely high BPM, making it the fastest single to reach number one.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: Anno Nefertari holds the world record for the fastest rap single that made it to number one, with their song \"1000 RPM.\" This track is known for its extremely high tempo, reaching approximately 200 BPM, and is the fastest single to achieve a number one chart position."}, "symptom": ["S3", "S4"], "symptom_span": "I think it's by Anno Nefertari, but I'm not sure."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-4ab51bdd80", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "How many member nations exist in the United Nations?", "q_trig": "How many member nations exist in the United Nations?", "answer": "193", "answer_aliases": ["193 members", "193 member states", "193 sovereign states"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "27, 2002.\n\nIt's worth noting that the Holy See (Vatican City) has permanent observer status at the UN, but is not a member state. The UN also has two observer states: the State of Palestine and the Holy See, which has permanent observer status.\n\nAdditionally, there are several territories and dependencies that are not member states but have a special relationship with the UN, such as Taiwan, which is officially known as the Republic of China, but is not recognized as a sovereign state by the UN.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "additionally several territories and organizations have permanent observer status at the un including the european union the international committee of the red cross and the international olympic committee among others ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many member nations are in the United Nations. I remember that the UN started a long time ago, maybe in the 1940s. I think it was established by some countries after World War II. I'm not exactly sure about the exact number, though. I think it's in the low thousands, maybe around 190 or so. \n\nWait, I think it's more than that. I remember learning that China joined later, so maybe after the original 10 or so. Then there were other countries that joined later, like India, which I believe joined in the 1950s. I think the number has been increasing each year as more countries became part of the UN. \n\nI also recall that there are some countries that are observer members, meaning they aren't official members but can participate in certain ways. For example, there are five veto-playing countries, which are called the Security Council members. These include China, the US, Russia, France, and the UK. So, the total number of member states would be the regular members plus these five. \n\nI think the current total is around 193 member states. That includes all the original members, the ones that joined later, and the observer countries. But I'm not 100% sure. Maybe I should think about how many countries have joined since the 1940s. \n\nI know that the UN started with 5 members: the US, UK, France, China, and Russia. Then, after the Cold War, many more joined, especially from the newly independent countries. Today, there are 193 member states, which includes all the original 5 plus a lot more. \n\nSo, putting it all together, the United Nations has 193 member nations. That includes the five permanent Security Council members who have the right to veto decisions. Therefore, the total number of member states is 193.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The United Nations has 193 member nations."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S4"], "symptom_span": "I think it's in the low thousands, maybe around 190 or so."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-20fea9ba79", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "Which is the oldest still-running university in the world?", "q_trig": "Which is the oldest still-running university in the world?", "answer": "University of Bologna", "answer_aliases": ["University of Bologna "], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": " programs in fields such as Islamic studies, Arabic language and literature, and traditional medicine.\n\nIt's worth noting that while the University of Al-Karaouine is the oldest continuously operating university, there were other ancient institutions of higher learning that existed before it, such as the ancient Library of Alexandria and the Academy of Athens. However, these institutions did not survive to the present day, whereas the University of Al-Karaouine has endured for over a millennium.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "so while there may be some debate about which university is the oldest al-karaouine university in morocco is generally recognized as the oldest continuously operating university in the world ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out which is the oldest still-running university in the world. I remember that universities have a long history, but I'm not exactly sure which one is still going strong and is the oldest. Let me think about this step by step.\n\nFirst, I know that many universities were established in the Middle Ages, so maybe one from that time is still running. I've heard of Oxford and Cambridge, but I think they were founded in the 12th and 13th centuries, respectively. But I'm not sure if they're still running. Wait, I think they are, but I should verify that.\n\nThen there's the University of Bologna, which I believe was one of the first. It was established in 1364, so that's pretty old. But is it still running? I think it is, but I'm not certain. There's also the University of Leiden in the Netherlands, founded in 1575, but I'm not sure if it's still active.\n\nI should also consider other universities from different regions. For example, the University of Bologna is in Italy, and I think it's still going. But maybe there are others in other countries that are older. Wait, the University of Padua was founded in 1364 as well, so that's another candidate. But I'm not sure if it's still running.\n\nI'm a bit confused because I've heard that some universities were closed or dissolved over the years, especially during times of conflict or political changes. For example, the University of Leiden was nationalized during World War II, so it might not be considered as a traditional university anymore. Similarly, some Eastern European universities were restructured after the fall of communism.\n\nSo, focusing on the ones that are still operational, I think the University of Bologna is a strong contender. It's one of the oldest and has a rich history. But I should check if there are any other universities that are older and still running.\n\nWait, the University of Padua was founded in 1364, same as Bologna, but I'm not sure if it's still active. I think it's still running, but I'm not certain. Also, there's the University of Coimbra in Portugal, founded in 1290, which is even older than Bologna and Padua, but I'm not sure if it's still operational.\n\nI should also consider the time frame. If the question is about the oldest still-running university, then it's probably one established in the 14th century. Coimbra was founded in 1290, which is earlier than Bologna and Padua. But I'm not sure if it's still running today.\n\nAnother thought: some universities were restructured or merged, so maybe they're not considered traditional universities anymore. For example, the University of Leiden was nationalized, so it's not a university in the traditional sense anymore. Similarly, some Eastern European universities were reorganized after the Soviet era.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the University of Bologna, established in 1364, is one of the oldest still-running universities. The University of Leiden, although older, might not be considered a traditional university anymore. The University of Coimbra is even older but I'm not sure about its current status.\n\nI think the University of Bologna is the most likely candidate because it's well-known and has a continuous history. But I'm still a bit unsure about the University of Coimbra. Maybe I should look up some sources to confirm which one is the oldest still-running university.\n\nAfter a quick check, it seems that the University of Coimbra was nationalized in 1974, so it's not considered a university anymore. Therefore, the University of Bologna stands out as the oldest still-running university. The University of Leiden was nationalized in 1970, so it's also not a traditional university anymore. The University of Padua is still operational, but it's not as old as Bologna.\n\nWait, no, the University of Padua was founded in 1364, same as Bologna, but I think it's still running. So, both Bologna and Padua are from the 14th century. But I'm not sure if they're both considered as the oldest. Maybe Bologna is more famous, but both are equally old.\n\nI think the answer is the University of Bologna, but I'm not entirely certain. There might be others, but Bologna is the most well-known and still running. So, I'll go with that.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The University of Bologna is the oldest still-running university in the world, established in 1364."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "University of Bologna, established in 1364, so that's pretty old."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-a8f7de675d", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "Who holds record for highest goals scored in international football for Malaysia?", "q_trig": "Who holds record for highest goals scored in international football for Malaysia?", "answer": "Mokhtar Dahari", "answer_aliases": ["Dahari", "Datuk Mohd Mokhtar Dahari"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "I don't have the most current information on the record for the highest goals scored in international football for Malaysia. My data might be outdated, and I recommend checking the latest updates from a reliable sports database or the official FIFA website for the most accurate and up-to-date information.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "i don t have the most current information on the record for the highest goals scored in international football for malaysia my data might be outdated and i recommend checking the latest updates from a reliable sports database or the official fédération internationale de football association fifa website for the most accurate and up-to-date information ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out who holds the record for the highest goals scored in international football for Malaysia. I'm not too familiar with their national team statistics, but I can try to piece this together.\n\nFirst, I know that Malaysia's national football team has participated in various international tournaments, like the Asian Cup and the Asian Cup for Under-23s. I remember that in the Asian Cup, the top scorers are usually highlighted, so maybe someone from there is the record holder.\n\nI think the record might be held by a striker, as strikers often score the most goals. Let me try to recall or think of any names. I believe it's either Siti Afiin or Nurul Izzati. I'm not sure which one it is, but I think they both have high numbers.\n\nWait, I think Siti Afiin was part of the 2002 Asian Cup team. She scored a lot of goals, maybe around 10 or more. I'm not certain about the exact number, but I remember her name being associated with that tournament.\n\nOn the other hand, Nurul Izzati might have been part of the 2006 Asian Cup. She was a very talented player, but I'm not sure if she scored as many goals as Siti Afiin. Maybe she scored around 8 or 9 goals.\n\nI should also consider if there are any other players who might have more goals. For example, have any other players from Malaysia scored more than 10 goals in international matches? I don't recall any names off the top of my head, but it's possible.\n\nAnother angle is to think about the context of the tournaments. The 2002 Asian Cup was a friendly tournament, but it's still considered a significant achievement. Siti Afiin's performance there might have been exceptional, leading her to have a higher goal count.\n\nAlso, considering the age of the players, Siti Afiin was part of the 1998 FIFA Women's World Cup, which might have influenced her playing career and goal-scoring record.\n\nI'm trying to remember if there are any records or articles that mention her as the top scorer. I think she is often cited as the top scorer for Malaysia in international football, but I'm not 100% sure about the exact number of goals.\n\nIn summary, I'm leaning towards Siti Afiin being the record holder, but I'm not entirely certain. I should probably look up the exact number of goals she scored and compare it with Nurul Izzati's record to confirm who has the higher number.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: Siti Afiin holds the record for the highest goals scored in international football for Malaysia, with a total of 10 goals in the 2002 Asian Cup."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "Siti Afiin was part of the 2002 Asian Cup team. She scored a lot of goals, maybe around 10 or more."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-64d6161f7a", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "Where is Tesla's headquarters?", "q_trig": "Where is Tesla's headquarters?", "answer": "Austin, TX", "answer_aliases": ["Austin"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "Tesla, Inc.'s headquarters is located in Palo Alto, California, United States.", "consistency": 0.5, "mode_answer": "tesla inc s headquarters is located in palo alto california united states ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out where Tesla's headquarters is located. I remember hearing that Tesla is a big company, especially known for electric cars. I think they're based in the US, but I'm not exactly sure about the exact address. I believe it's in California because that's a common tech hub. Maybe it's in a city like San Francisco or somewhere nearby. I've heard of a company called NIO, which I think is a competitor to Tesla, and I believe they're based in China. But Tesla must have their own headquarters somewhere in the US. I'm trying to recall if it's in a specific city or maybe a town. I think it's in California, perhaps in a place like Fremont or maybe Palo Alto. Wait, no, I think it's actually in a place called Tesla City, but I'm not certain. Maybe it's in a city like Mountain View or Stanford? I'm a bit confused now. I should probably look it up to confirm, but since I can't right now, I'll try to piece it together. Tesla is a leader in electric vehicles, so their headquarters would be in a place conducive to that, maybe near universities or tech parks. California has a lot of tech companies, so that makes sense. I think the headquarters is in a city called Fremont, but I'm not 100% sure. Alternatively, it could be in Palo Alto, which is near Stanford University. Hmm, I'm a bit stuck here. I think I'll go with Fremont as the headquarters, but I'm not entirely certain. Maybe I should double-check that.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: Tesla's headquarters is located in Fremont, California."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I think it's in a place like Fremont or maybe Palo Alto."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-b1451fb006", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "The longest unbeaten streak of all time in the Premier League is how many matches?", "q_trig": "The longest unbeaten streak of all time in the Premier League is how many matches?", "answer": "49", "answer_aliases": ["forty-nine", "49 matches", "49 matches by Arsenal FC"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "The longest unbeaten streak in the Premier League is held by Manchester City, who went 28 matches without a loss from December 2017 to May 2018.", "consistency": 0.25, "mode_answer": "i don t have the information on the longest unbeaten streak in the premier league ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out what the longest unbeaten streak in the Premier League history is. I'm not too familiar with all the teams and their histories, but I know the Premier League is the top English football league. Let me think about the teams that have had long unbeaten streaks.\n\nFirst, I remember that Manchester United had a really long run. I think they went a while without losing, maybe even a season or more. But I'm not sure exactly how long. Then there's Chelsea, which I think had a very long unbeaten streak as well, maybe even longer than United.\n\nWait, I think Chelsea might have had a streak that went beyond 100 matches. I'm trying to recall if it was around 115 or something like that. But I'm not certain. Maybe I should break it down by season or something.\n\nAlso, there's the concept of a \"clutch\" or \"streak\" in football, where teams go on a winning run without losing. I think the record is held by either Manchester United or Chelsea, but I'm not sure which one. Maybe I should check the timeline of when these teams were playing.\n\nAnother thing to consider is that the Premier League has expanded over the years. Originally, it had 22 teams, but now it's 20. So, the teams that have had the longest streaks might have been around since the league was established, maybe even before it expanded.\n\nI think the current Premier League started in 1992-93, but the history goes back further. So, some teams that were in the league before 1992 might have longer unbeaten streaks. For example, Manchester United was in the league since its inception, so they might have a longer streak.\n\nBut I'm not sure about the exact number. I think Chelsea's streak was around 115 matches, but I'm not certain. Maybe I should think about how many seasons that is. If each season is 38 matches, then 115 divided by 38 is roughly 3 years. So, a 3-year unbeaten streak would be about 115 matches. That seems really long, but I think it's correct.\n\nWait, I also remember something about a team going 116 matches without a loss. Was that Chelsea? Or was it Manchester United? I think Chelsea might have had that. But I'm not 100% sure.\n\nAnother point is that sometimes teams have back-to-back wins or draws, which can extend their unbeaten streak. So, if a team wins multiple games in a row, that counts towards the streak.\n\nI should also consider that the Premier League has had different numbers of teams over the years. Before 2012, it was 20 teams, but before that, it was 18. So, the teams that have had the longest streaks might have been around when there were fewer teams, allowing for more matches without a loss.\n\nWait, but the question is about the longest unbeaten streak ever, so it's not about the longest winning streak, but the longest without a loss. That's a bit different. So, a team could have a long winning streak but also include draws, which would make the unbeaten streak longer.\n\nI think Chelsea's 115-match unbeaten streak included a lot of draws, especially in the early part of their history. So, that might be the record. But I'm not entirely sure if it's Chelsea or Manchester United.\n\nLet me try to recall specific seasons. Manchester United had a long unbeaten streak in the late 90s and early 2000s. They were in the league for a long time, so they might have had a streak around 100 matches. Chelsea, on the other hand, joined the Premier League in 1992, so they've had more time to build up a long streak.\n\nI think Chelsea's record is the 115-match unbeaten streak, which is the longest ever in the Premier League. But I'm not 100% certain. I should probably verify this, but since I can't look it up right now, I'll go with what I remember.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the longest unbeaten streak in the Premier League history is 115 matches, held by Chelsea FC. They achieved this without losing since the 1992-93 season. That's an incredible run!\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The longest unbeaten streak in the Premier League history is 115 matches, held by Chelsea FC since the 1992-93 season."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I think Chelsea might have had a streak that went beyond 100 matches."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-2e6d260ed6", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "Which language has the third largest population by total number of speakers?", "q_trig": "Which language has the third largest population by total number of speakers?", "answer": "Hindi", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "According to Ethnologue, a reliable source for language statistics, the top 3 languages by total number of speakers are:\n\n1. **Mandarin Chinese**: 1.3 billion speakers\n2. **Spanish**: 460 million speakers\n3. **English**: 379 million speakers\n\nSo, the answer is **English**.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "the language with the third largest population by total number of speakers is english with approximately 983 million native speakers and over 1 5 billion total speakers native and non-native ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out which language has the third largest population by the total number of speakers. Hmm, I'm not exactly sure about the exact numbers, but I remember that Mandarin Chinese is the most spoken, followed by English. Then, I think about other languages that are widely spoken around the world. \n\nI recall that Spanish is spoken in many countries, especially in Latin America. Maybe it's the third? Or perhaps it's another language. I'm not certain. I should probably think about the regions where languages are dominant. For example, in India, many regional languages are spoken, but I don't know if they surpass other languages in terms of total speakers.\n\nWait, I think about the United Nations data or some reports on language demographics. I remember that English is definitely number one, and Mandarin is number two. So, the third would be somewhere else. Maybe it's a language that's prevalent in a large number of countries or has a massive native speaker base.\n\nI'm trying to recall if it's Spanish, but I'm not sure if it's the third. Maybe it's another language. I think about languages like Arabic, but I believe Arabic is more spoken in specific regions rather than globally. Similarly, languages like Hindi or Russian might be more spoken in certain areas but not the third overall.\n\nWait, I think I've heard that English is first, Mandarin is second, and then maybe it's a language like Spanish or another Romance language. But I'm not certain. I should try to think of the approximate numbers. If Mandarin is around 1.2 billion speakers, English is about 1 billion, then the third might be Spanish with around 400 million. But I'm not sure if that's accurate.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's a language like Arabic, but I think it's more concentrated in specific regions. Or perhaps it's a language like Bengali, which is spoken in Bangladesh and some neighboring countries. But again, I'm not sure about the exact numbers.\n\nI think I need to look up some approximate figures. Mandarin is about 1.2 billion, English is about 1 billion, and then the next would be a language that's widely spoken in many countries. Spanish comes to mind because it's spoken in many Latin American countries, but I'm not sure if it's the third. Maybe it's a language like Hindi, which is spoken in India, but I think Hindi is more than 100 million speakers, so maybe it's not the third.\n\nWait, I think I remember that the third is actually a language like Arabic, but I'm not sure. Or maybe it's a language like Russian, but I think it's more in specific regions. Hmm, this is a bit confusing.\n\nI think I should try to recall if I've heard any specific reports. I believe that the third largest is actually a language like Arabic, but I'm not certain. Alternatively, maybe it's a language like Hindi, but I think Hindi is more than 100 million speakers, so it might be higher than Arabic.\n\nWait, I think I'm mixing up the order. Maybe the third is actually a language like Arabic, but I'm not sure. Alternatively, maybe it's a language like Bengali, which is spoken in Bangladesh and some other countries. But I'm not certain about the exact numbers.\n\nI think I need to make an educated guess. If Mandarin is first, English is second, then the third would likely be a language that's widely spoken in many countries, like Spanish or Arabic. But I'm not sure which one is higher. I think Spanish is more widely spoken globally than Arabic, so maybe Spanish is the third.\n\nBut I'm not entirely confident. I think I should check some sources or data to confirm, but since I can't do that right now, I'll have to go with my best guess. So, I'll say that Spanish is the third largest by total number of speakers.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: Spanish is the third largest language by total number of speakers."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S4"], "symptom_span": "Spanish is the third largest by total number of speakers."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-92e9cf2205", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "Which is the second largest language edition of Wikipedia according to article count?", "q_trig": "Which is the second largest language edition of Wikipedia according to article count?", "answer": "Cebuano", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "According to the Wikipedia statistics, the second largest language edition of Wikipedia is the German Wikipedia, with over 2.3 million articles.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "note that these numbers are subject to change over time but the german wikipedia has consistently been the second-largest language edition of wikipedia for many years ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out which language edition of Wikipedia has the second largest number of articles. I know that the main language, English, is the largest, but I'm not exactly sure about the others. I remember hearing that Chinese is a big one, but I'm not certain if it's second or maybe even larger than English. \n\nI think I should start by recalling what I know about Wikipedia's language editions. I remember that the number of articles varies by language, and it's influenced by factors like population, the importance of the language in the country, and cultural factors. For example, Chinese is spoken by a large population, so it's likely to have a lot of articles. \n\nI also recall that the Russian edition is quite extensive, maybe even larger than Chinese. But I'm not sure if it's second or if another language is bigger. Maybe something like Arabic or Spanish? Spanish is a major language in many countries, so perhaps it's close to Chinese in terms of article count.\n\nI should also consider the growth of Wikipedia in different languages. For instance, some smaller languages might have grown rapidly in recent years, but I don't think they've overtaken the top two. The growth of the Chinese edition, especially after the government's support and the large population, makes me think it's a strong contender for second place.\n\nI wonder if there are any statistics or reports that list the top language editions by article count. Maybe I can think of the order I've heard before. I think it's something like English first, then Chinese, then Russian, followed by Arabic, Spanish, etc. So, if that's the case, the second largest would be Chinese. But I'm not 100% certain.\n\nAnother angle is to think about the number of active editors or the number of pages in each language. If Chinese has the most active editors, it might have the most articles. But I'm not sure if that's directly related. \n\nI also remember that the number of articles can vary over time, so it's possible that the order changes as more people contribute. But as of the latest data I have, I think Chinese remains the second largest. \n\nSo, putting it all together, I'm leaning towards Chinese being the second largest language edition of Wikipedia in terms of article count. English is first, followed by Chinese, then Russian, and so on. Therefore, the answer should be Chinese.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The second largest language edition of Wikipedia, according to article count, is Chinese."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I think I should start by recalling what I know about Wikipedia's language editions."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-3404336d78", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "Which K-Pop artist has the most followers on Instagram?", "q_trig": "Which K-Pop artist has the most followers on Instagram?", "answer": "Lisa (BLACKPINK)", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "se numbers are subject to change rapidly and may not reflect the current numbers.\n\nHere are the top 5 K-Pop artists with the most followers on Instagram:\n\n1. **Jimin (BTS)** - over 80 million followers\n2. **BTS (official account)** - over 70 million followers\n3. **Blackpink** - over 60 million followers\n4. **BTS's V** - over 50 million followers\n5. **BTS's Jungkook** - over 45 million followers\n\nPlease keep in mind that these numbers are subject to change and may not reflect the current numbers.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "please keep in mind that these numbers are subject to change and may not reflect the current numbers ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out which K-Pop artist has the most Instagram followers. I'm not too familiar with all the K-Pop stars out there, but I know some of the big names. I remember seeing some of them on Instagram, but I'm not sure who leads in terms of follower count. \n\nFirst, I should probably think about the most popular K-Pop groups. There's BTS, right? They're super popular. I think they have a huge following, but I'm not sure if individual members have more followers than the group itself. Then there'sBLACKPINK, who are part of BTS, so maybe they're closely related in terms of popularity.\n\nI've also heard about groups like J net, V, and Snoop Dogg. Wait, Snoop Dogg is actually a solo artist, but he's part of a K-Pop group, so maybe he's considered a K-Pop artist. I'm not sure about his Instagram numbers though. \n\nThen there are solo artists like Jung Ho-yeon from f(x) or Oh Hye-nye from Miss A. I think f(x) is a well-known girl group, but I'm not sure how their members compare in terms of Instagram followers. \n\nI should also consider whether the main members of a group have more followers than the group itself. I think in some cases, the lead member of a group might have more individual following because they're more visible on social media. \n\nI remember seeing some numbers somewhere, but I'm not sure if they're accurate. For example, I think BTS's members have over 100 million followers each, but the group as a whole might have around 200 million combined. So maybe individually, they have more. \n\nBLACKPINK's members are part of BTS, so they might share some of the same follower base. I'm not sure if their individual numbers are higher or lower than BTS's members. \n\nThen there's Snoop Dogg. I think he's got a significant following, but I'm not sure if it's in the same league as the main K-Pop artists. Maybe he's around 50 million followers or something like that. \n\nJung Ho-yeon from f(x) is another solo artist I've heard about. I think she's got a decent following, maybe around 20 million or so. \n\nOh Hye-nye from Miss A is also a solo artist, and I believe she's got a following in the tens of millions. \n\nSo, putting this all together, it seems like BTS's members are leading in terms of Instagram followers. Each of them individually has over 100 million followers, which is more than the group's total. So, the answer would be one of the main members of BTS, like Jung Ho-yeon or others. \n\nWait, but I'm not entirely sure if the numbers I'm recalling are accurate. Maybe I should double-check, but since I can't access external sources, I'll go with what I know. \n\nSo, to sum up, the most follower-heavy Instagram accounts in K-Pop are likely the main members of BTS, each with over 100 million followers. So, the answer would be one of them, but since the question asks for the artist, it's probably referring to the group as a whole, but individually, they have the most.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The K-Pop artist with the most Instagram followers is a member of BTS, with each main member having over 100 million followers."}, "symptom": ["S3", "S1"], "symptom_span": "I think they have a huge following, but I'm not sure if individual members have more followers than the group itself."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-c6af044284", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "Which is the most recently launched NASA space telescope that observes in the visible and near-infrared?", "q_trig": "Which is the most recently launched NASA space telescope that observes in the visible and near-infrared?", "answer": "Pandora", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "The most recently launched NASA space telescope that observes in the visible and near-infrared is the James Webb Space Telescope (JWST). It was launched on December 25, 2021, aboard an Ariane 5 rocket from the Guiana Space Centre in French Guiana.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "the jwst is the most complex and ambitious space telescope ever built with a sunshield that must be kept at extremely low temperatures to function properly and a segmented primary mirror that must be precisely aligned to form a sharp image it is a remarkable achievement of engineering and scientific collaboration and it is expected to make many groundbreaking discoveries in the coming years ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out which is the most recently launched NASA space telescope that observes in the visible and near-infrared. Hmm, I'm not super familiar with all the recent space telescopes, but I'll try to think it through.\n\nFirst, I remember that NASA has a few major space telescopes. There's the Hubble Space Telescope, but I think it's older now. I believe it was launched in the 90s and is still operational, but I'm not sure if it's the most recent. Then there's the James Webb Space Telescope, which I think is the latest one, but I'm not sure what wavelengths it observes in. I think it's infrared, but does it cover visible light too?\n\nWait, the user is asking specifically about a telescope that observes in the visible and near-infrared. So maybe the Hubble isn't the one because I think it's more focused on infrared and visible, but I'm not certain. Let me think about other telescopes. There's also the Euclid telescope, but I think that was launched a few years ago, maybe in the early 2020s. I'm not sure if it's the most recent.\n\nThen there's the Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope, which I believe is the successor to the Hubble. It's supposed to be launched in the near future, maybe 2024. If that's the case, then it would be more recent than Euclid. But I'm not sure if it's operational yet or if it's still in the planning phase.\n\nWait, the James Webb Space Telescope was launched in 2021, right? It's the latest one, but I'm not sure if it's visible or near-infrared. I think it's mostly infrared, but maybe it has some visible capabilities. I should check that. If the James Webb doesn't observe in the visible and near-infrared, then maybe the next one is the Nancy Grace Roman.\n\nSo, putting it together: Hubble is old, Euclid was in 2020s, Roman is planned for 2024. If Roman is the most recent, then that's the answer. But I'm not 100% sure about the wavelengths each observes. I think Hubble does a bit of visible, Euclid is near-infrared, and Roman is designed for optical and near-infrared. So maybe the Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope is the most recent one that observes in visible and near-infrared.\n\nI should also consider if there are any other telescopes in between. Like, maybe the WFIRST mission, which was a NASA mission that's now part of the Nancy Grace Roman. So WFIRST was launched in 2013, but it's no longer operational. So the most recent one would be the Roman, which is in the works.\n\nSo, to sum up, the most recently launched (planned) NASA space telescope that observes in visible and near-infrared would be the Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope, scheduled for launch in 2024.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The most recently launched (planned) NASA space telescope that observes in the visible and near-infrared wavelengths is the Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope, which is scheduled for launch in 2024."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "Hubble is old, Euclid was in 2020s, Roman is planned for 2024."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-02d5787535", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "How many member states are there in the Eurozone?", "q_trig": "How many member states are there in the Eurozone?", "answer": "21", "answer_aliases": ["twenty-one"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "pted the Euro as their official currency and are required to meet certain economic criteria to participate in the Eurozone.\n\nThe 19 member states of the Eurozone are:\n\n1. Austria\n2. Belgium\n3. Cyprus\n4. Estonia\n5. Finland\n6. France\n7. Germany\n8. Greece\n9. Ireland\n10. Italy\n11. Latvia\n12. Lithuania\n13. Luxembourg\n14. Malta\n15. the Netherlands\n16. Portugal\n17. Slovakia\n18. Slovenia\n19. Spain\n\nNote that the United Kingdom (UK) was a member of the EU but not the Eurozone, and it left the EU in 2020.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "as of my last update in 2023 there are 20 member states in the eurozone the eurozone also known as the euro area is a subset of the european union eu where the official currency is the euro ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many member states are in the Eurozone. I'm not entirely sure, but I know it's a group of European countries that use the euro as their currency. I remember hearing about some countries being part of it, but I'm not clear on the exact number. Let me try to break this down.\n\nFirst, I think the Eurozone includes all the European Union countries that have adopted the euro. The European Union has a varying number of members, but I believe it's around 27 now. But not all of them are in the Eurozone. I think some countries joined later or didn't join at all. For example, I remember Ireland and Portugal joining relatively recently. Maybe there are a few countries that haven't joined yet.\n\nI should also consider historical context. The euro was introduced in 1999, so maybe some countries joined before that? Wait, no, the euro was part of the Maastricht Treaty, which was signed in 1997, but the actual implementation happened in 1999. So countries joined either before or after that. I think some joined before, like Germany, France, Italy, and Spain, which were part of the European Economic Community earlier. Others joined later, like Ireland, Greece, Portugal, and maybe some others.\n\nI'm trying to recall specific countries. Let's see: Germany, France, Italy, Spain, the Netherlands, Belgium, Luxembourg, Austria, Switzerland, Denmark, Finland, Sweden, Norway, Portugal, Greece, Hungary, Slovakia, Czech Republic, Poland, Lithuania, Belarus, Latvia, Estonia, Lithuania, Bosnia and Herzegovina, Albania, North Macedonia, Romania, Bulgaria, Greece, Cyprus, Malta, and Greece again? Wait, that seems like a lot. Maybe I'm double-counting or including countries that aren't part of the Eurozone.\n\nWait, I think some countries like Malta and Cyprus are part of the Eurozone, but I'm not sure if they're considered separate or if they're part of the EU. Also, countries like Ukraine, which used to be part of the Soviet Union, are now part of the European Union but not the Eurozone. So they're not included.\n\nLet me try to list them out:\n\n1. Germany\n2. France\n3. Italy\n4. Spain\n5. Netherlands\n6. Belgium\n7. Luxembourg\n8. Austria\n9. Switzerland\n10. Denmark\n11. Finland\n12. Sweden\n13. Norway\n14. Portugal\n15. Greece\n16. Hungary\n17. Slovakia\n18. Czech Republic\n19. Poland\n20. Lithuania\n21. Belarus\n22. Latvia\n23. Estonia\n24. Malta\n25. Cyprus\n\nWait, that's 25. But I think some of these might not be correct. For example, Malta and Cyprus are part of the Eurozone, but I'm not sure if they're considered separate or if they're included in the 27 EU countries. Also, some countries like Romania, Bulgaria, etc., are part of the EU but might not have joined the Eurozone yet.\n\nI think the current number is around 19 or 20. Let me check my memory: Germany, France, Italy, Spain, the Netherlands, Belgium, Luxembourg, Austria, Switzerland, Denmark, Finland, Sweden, Norway, Portugal, Greece, Hungary, Slovakia, Czech Republic, Poland, Lithuania, Belarus, Latvia, Estonia, Malta, Cyprus. That's 25, but I think some of these joined after 1999, so they are part of the Eurozone now.\n\nWait, I'm getting confused. Maybe I should think about the current list. As of now, the Eurozone includes 19 countries. Let me see: Germany, France, Italy, Spain, the Netherlands, Belgium, Luxembourg, Austria, Switzerland, Denmark, Finland, Sweden, Norway, Portugal, Greece, Hungary, Slovakia, Czech Republic, Poland, Lithuania. That's 19. So maybe the answer is 19.\n\nBut I'm not entirely sure. I think some sources say 19, others might include more. I should probably look up the current list, but since I can't do that right now, I'll go with my best estimate. I think the Eurozone has 19 member states.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: There are 19 member states in the Eurozone."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "The European Union has a varying number of members, but I believe it's around 27 now."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-a8c02b0182", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "What country became the latest country to adopt the Euro?", "q_trig": "What country became the latest country to adopt the Euro?", "answer": "Bulgaria", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "e are ongoing discussions and negotiations about potential future adoptions, particularly among countries in the Western Balkans and other regions. Some of these countries have expressed interest in joining the Eurozone in the future, but no official announcements have been made regarding any new countries adopting the Euro.\n\nIf you're looking for the most up-to-date information, I recommend checking the latest news and official announcements from the European Union or the European Central Bank.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "if you re interested in staying up-to-date on eurozone news and developments i d be happy to help ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out which country became the latest to adopt the Euro. I know that the Euro is the currency used by several European countries, but I'm not exactly sure about the order in which countries joined. Let me think about this step by step.\n\nFirst, I remember that the Euro was introduced in 1999, but not all countries joined immediately. Some countries were part of the Maastricht Agreement, which was the first step, and others joined later under the €100 billion agreement. I think Germany was one of the first to join, maybe around 1999. Then, I believe France and Italy joined around the same time.\n\nI'm trying to recall the timeline. I think Spain joined in 1999 as well. Maybe the next countries to join were the Netherlands, Belgium, and maybe some others. I'm not sure about the exact order. I think Sweden might have joined later, perhaps in 2000. Then, countries like Portugal, Greece, and others might have followed.\n\nWait, I'm a bit confused. I think the last country to join was either Estonia, Latvia, or Lithuania. I believe it was Estonia that joined the Euro in 2009. So, up to 2009, most of the EU countries were part of the Eurozone, but a few held out longer.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the Euro was adopted by Germany, France, Italy, Spain, the Netherlands, Belgium, Luxembourg, and maybe some others before 2009. The last country to join was Estonia in 2009. Therefore, the latest country to adopt the Euro would be Estonia.\n\nI should double-check this to make sure. I recall that the Euro was introduced in 1999 for the Maastricht Agreement, which included most EU countries except those that were too small or had economic issues. The €100 billion agreement was later, and that included more countries, with the last one being Estonia in 2009. So yes, Estonia was the last country to join the Eurozone.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: Estonia was the latest country to adopt the Euro, joining in 2009."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I think the last country to join was either Estonia, Latvia, or Lithuania."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-2b15f422e8", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "What was the Weeknd's last studio album titled?", "q_trig": "What was the Weeknd's last studio album titled?", "answer": "Hurry Up Tomorrow", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "The Weeknd's last studio album was \"Dawn FM\", which was released on January 7, 2022.", "consistency": 0.5, "mode_answer": "the weeknd s last studio album was dawn fm which was released on january 7 2022 ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out what the Weeknd's last studio album was called. I'm not super familiar with his discography, but I know he's a pretty big artist, so I can probably piece this together.\n\nFirst, I remember that he's had several albums over the years. I think his latest one might be from a few years back. I'm trying to recall if it was released in 2021 or 2022. I think it was 2021 because I've heard a lot about it in the news around that time.\n\nHis earlier albums were more about his music before he became more prominent. I think his first album was called \"After That.\" Then he did \"Before and After,\" which was his second album. After that, he released \"This Is Not the Time,\" which was his third. Then \"A New Day\" followed, which was his fourth. \n\nI believe his fifth album was \"In the Name of Love,\" which came out in 2018. After that, he did \"The One,\" which was his sixth album, released in 2020. So, the next one would logically be his seventh album. Since I think he released something in 2021, that would make it his seventh album.\n\nI'm trying to remember the exact title. I think it was something like \"Mars.\" Yeah, that sounds right. So, his last studio album would be \"Mars.\" I'm pretty sure that's correct, but I should double-check to make sure I'm not mixing up the release years or the album titles.\n\nWait, let me think again. \"After That\" was 2010, \"Before and After\" 2011, \"This Is Not the Time\" 2013, \"A New Day\" 2014, \"In the Name of Love\" 2018, \"The One\" 2020, and then \"Mars\" in 2021. Yeah, that seems to fit. So, the last studio album is definitely \"Mars.\"\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The Weeknd's last studio album was titled \"Mars.\""}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I think his latest one might be from a few years back."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-60b95cc625", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "How many children does Elon Musk have, including his deceased child?", "q_trig": "How many children does Elon Musk have, including his deceased child?", "answer": "14", "answer_aliases": ["fourteen", "at least 14", "at least fourteen"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "0)\n7. Sashka Musk (born 2010)\n8. X Æ A-Xii Musk (born 2020)\n9. Exa Dark Sideræl Musk (born 2022)\n10. Shivon Zilis Musk (born 2022) - note: Shivon Zilis is a Canadian businesswoman and a close associate of Elon Musk, and it is reported that she gave birth to twins with him in 2022.\n\nIt's worth noting that Elon Musk has also been involved in the lives of his children from his previous relationships, including his children with Justine Musk (now Justine Musk Wilson), with whom he has five children.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "so to summarize elon musk has 10 children including his deceased child nevada alexander musk ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many children Elon Musk has, including his deceased child. I'm not too familiar with his personal life, but I remember he's a well-known figure, especially in the tech industry. Let me start by recalling what I know.\n\nI think Elon Musk is the CEO of Tesla and SpaceX, right? I've heard he's involved in a lot of interesting projects, both successful and maybe some that haven't worked out. Now, when it comes to his family life, I'm not as sure. I believe he's married to someone, maybe his partner is his spouse. I think his name is Krasiya, but I'm not 100% certain about that. So, they might have had some children together.\n\nI remember reading somewhere that he has a few kids, but I'm not clear on the exact number. Was it two, three, or more? I think he might have had twins or something like that. Also, I believe one of his children passed away, maybe in a tragic accident. I think it was his daughter, but I'm not sure of her name. Maybe she was involved in a car accident or something similar.\n\nWait, I think she was named Nevaeh. I'm not entirely sure, but I think that's the name. So, if she's deceased, that would mean she's not counted anymore. So, the children he has now would be the ones born after her passing. I'm trying to remember if he has any other children besides her. I think he might have had another child, maybe a son, but I'm not certain.\n\nPutting this together, I think he has two children alive today, but originally, he had three children, with one passing away. So, the total number including the deceased would be three. But I'm a bit confused because I'm not 100% sure about the names and the exact number. Maybe I should double-check this information to be accurate.\n\nWait, I think I might be mixing up some details. Let me try to piece it together. He's married to someone, they have children, one of whom is deceased. I believe the deceased child is a daughter, and she was named Nevaeh. So, if she's gone, and he has another child, maybe a son, then the total would be two children alive. But the question is asking for the total including the deceased, so that would be three.\n\nI'm trying to remember if there are any other children. I don't think so. So, Elon Musk is married to Krasiya Musk, and they have three children: Nevaeh, who is deceased, and two others, maybe a son and another daughter. But I'm not sure about the exact number of children. Maybe I should look up a reliable source to confirm this, but since I can't do that right now, I'll go with what I remember.\n\nSo, to sum up, Elon Musk is married to Krasiya Musk, and they have three children in total. One of them, Nevaeh, passed away, so she's not alive anymore. Therefore, the total number of children he has, including the deceased one, is three. But I'm a bit uncertain about the exact names and the number, so I might be mixing up some details. However, based on what I recall, that's the most plausible answer.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: Elon Musk has three children in total, including his deceased child. His children are Nevaeh, who passed away, and two others, likely a son and another daughter."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I think his name is Krasiya, but I'm not 100% certain about that."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-51836ad203", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "How many seats are there in the German Bundestag?", "q_trig": "How many seats are there in the German Bundestag?", "answer": "630", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "There are 709 seats in the German Bundestag, the federal parliament of Germany.", "consistency": 0.25, "mode_answer": "there are 709 seats in the german bundestag the lower house of the german parliament ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many seats there are in the German Bundestag. I'm not too familiar with German politics, but I know the Bundestag is the parliament of Germany. Let me think about what I remember.\n\nI think the Bundestag is made up of members elected from various regions. Maybe it's similar to the US Congress or the UK Parliament. In the US, there are 435 representatives, but Germany's system might be different.\n\nWait, I remember something about the composition of the Bundestag. There are different types of members. There are regular members who are elected by the public, and then there are members from the government, like the Chancellor and members of the government. Also, there are members from the European Parliament, who are also part of the Bundestag.\n\nSo, if I break it down, the total number of seats should be the sum of these different groups. Let me try to recall the numbers. I think there are 135 regular members elected by the public. Then, there are 15 members from the government, including the Chancellor. Additionally, the European Parliament has 750 members, but only 5 of them are part of the Bundestag.\n\nSo, adding those up: 135 (public members) + 15 (government members) + 5 (European Parliament members) = 155 seats in total. That seems right because I've heard the number 155 before in the context of the German parliament.\n\nI should double-check to make sure I didn't mix up any numbers. The public members are indeed 135, which is a bit more than the 133 in the previous parliament. The government members are 15, which includes the Chancellor and 14 other ministers. The European Parliament's representation is 5, which is a fixed number based on the European Union's rules.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the total number of seats in the German Bundestag is 155.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: There are 155 seats in the German Bundestag, consisting of 135 public members, 15 government members, and 5 members from the European Parliament."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S4"], "symptom_span": "There are 135 regular members elected by the public, and then there are 15 members from the government, like the Chancellor and members of the government."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-5b5fe14d26", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": [], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "How many core UMass NLP faculty are currently primarily affiliated with the lab and have been there for more than five years?", "q_trig": "How many core UMass NLP faculty are currently primarily affiliated with the lab and have been there for more than five years?", "answer": "2", "answer_aliases": ["two"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "I don't have access to real-time data or specific information about the current faculty affiliations and tenure of the University of Massachusetts (UMass) NLP lab. For the most accurate and up-to-date information, I recommend checking the official UMass NLP lab website or contacting them directly.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "i don t have access to real-time or current data including specific information about the current faculty affiliations and tenure of researchers at the university of massachusetts umass nlp lab for the most accurate and up-to-date information i recommend checking the official umass nlp lab s website or contacting them directly ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many core UMass NLP faculty are currently primarily affiliated with the lab and have been there for more than five years. Hmm, I'm not exactly sure where to start, but I'll try to break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, I should understand what UMass NLP refers to. I think UMass stands for the University of Massachusetts, and NLP is Natural Language Processing, a field in computer science and linguistics. So, UMass NLP would be the Natural Language Processing group or lab at the University of Massachusetts.\n\nNow, the question is about core faculty members who are \"primarily affiliated\" with the lab and have been there for more than five years. \"Core\" probably means regular faculty members, not adjuncts or visitors. \"Primarily affiliated\" might mean they are full-time or have a significant role in the lab's activities. \"More than five years\" indicates that they have been at the lab for a considerable time, which might suggest they are long-term members and possibly have contributed significantly to the lab's work.\n\nI'm not sure how to get the exact number, but maybe I can think about how to approach this if I were to look it up. I would probably check the university's website, specifically the NLP lab's page, where they often list their current members. Alternatively, the faculty members' individual pages might have information about their tenure at the lab.\n\nAnother approach is to look at the lab's annual reports or any recent publications. Sometimes, authors of papers are listed, and if they're from UMass NLP, that could indicate their current affiliation. However, this might not be as reliable since some faculty might have joint appointments or be part of other projects.\n\nI should also consider that some faculty might have moved to other labs or universities, so their primary affiliation might have changed. But since the question specifies \"currently primarily affiliated,\" I can focus on those who are still active at UMass NLP.\n\nI wonder if there's a way to filter faculty members by their tenure. Maybe some directories or databases track faculty positions over time. If I had access to such a resource, I could filter for those who have been at UMass NLP for over five years and are core members.\n\nAnother thought: perhaps the lab maintains a list of its core members on their website, and that list would include their tenure. So, I could visit the UMass NLP lab's website and look for a faculty section or a list of members with their years of service.\n\nI should also consider that the number might have changed recently, so the figure could be approximate. Without access to real-time data, it's hard to give an exact number, but I can outline the steps one would take to find the answer if I were doing this research.\n\nIn summary, to find the answer, I would:\n\n1. Visit the UMass NLP lab's official website.\n2. Navigate to the faculty or people section.\n3. Look for a list of core faculty members.\n4. Check their bios or CVs to see how long they've been affiliated with the lab.\n5. Count those who have been there for more than five years and are primarily affiliated.\n\nSince I can't access the internet or specific websites, I can't provide the exact number, but this is the method I would use to determine it.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: To determine the number of core UMass NLP faculty currently primarily affiliated with the lab and who have been there for more than five years, one would typically follow these steps:\n\n1. **Visit the UMass NLP Lab's Official Website**: Access the website of the University of Massachusetts's Natural Language Processing (NLP) lab.\n\n2. **Navigate to the Faculty or People Section**: Look for a section dedicated to faculty members, researchers, or core members of the lab.\n\n3. **Review Core Faculty Members**: Examine the list of core faculty members, focusing on those who are regular or full-time members.\n\n4. **Check Tenure and Affiliation**: For each faculty member, review their bios or CVs to determine their tenure at the lab. Specifically, identify those who have been there for more than five years and are primarily affiliated.\n\n5. **Count the Eligible Faculty**: Tally the number of faculty members who meet the criteria of being core, primarily affiliated, and having a tenure of over five years.\n\nSince I cannot access current websites or specific data, I cannot provide the exact number. However, this method outlines the steps one would take to find the answer."}, "symptom": ["S3", "S4"], "symptom_span": "I'm not exactly sure where to start, but I'll try to break it down step by step."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-d14a865a1b", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "How many LA metro rail stations are there?", "q_trig": "How many LA metro rail stations are there?", "answer": "107", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": " that runs from Downtown Los Angeles to Santa Monica, passing through the Westside of Los Angeles. It is also known as the C Line.\n\n6. **Green Line**: This is a light rail line that runs from Norwalk to Redondo Beach, passing through the South Bay area. It is the newest line in the LA Metro system.\n\nEach of these lines has multiple stations, and the total number of stations across all lines is around 110. However, the exact number can fluctuate as new stations are added or as lines are extended.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "**total ** this adds up to 115 stations across all lines however it s worth noting that the la metro rail system is expanding with several projects under construction or in planning which will add more stations and lines ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many LA metro rail stations there are. I'm not exactly sure, but I'll try to work it out step by step. \n\nFirst, I know that the Los Angeles County Metropolitan Transportation District (Metro) operates the Los Angeles地铁 system. I've heard it's a pretty extensive network, covering a lot of the city. But I'm not sure about the exact number of stations. I think it's in the hundreds, maybe even over 500? But I'm not certain.\n\nI remember that the LA subway system includes the classic blue line, which I believe goes from Downtown to Culver City. I think that line has a lot of stations. Maybe around 100? I'm not sure, but I'll keep that in mind.\n\nThen there are other lines. I think there's the red line, which goes from North Hills to South Bay. I'm not sure how many stations that has, but I think it's a significant number too. Maybe another 50 or so?\n\nI also recall that there are newer stations being built or added, especially in response to population growth and new developments. For example, places like Culver City, Santa Monica, and even some areas outside the city proper, like Tujunga and Van Nuys, have stations now. That might add another 20-30 stations.\n\nThen there are the orange line extensions, like the one going to Santa Fe and the one that's being built to Van Nuys. Each of those probably has several stations. Maybe 10 each? That would add another 20.\n\nI also think about the newer systems, like the Metrorail Blue Line, which is the newer version of the blue line. I believe it has about 100 stations, but I'm not sure if that's accurate.\n\nWait, I'm getting a bit confused. Let me try to break it down by lines:\n\n1. Blue Line: Original line from Downtown to Culver City. I think it has around 100 stations, including the newer ones like those in Culver City and Santa Monica.\n\n2. Red Line: From North Hills to South Bay. I believe it has about 50 stations, maybe more, including newer ones in Culver City and Santa Monica.\n\n3. Orange Line: Originally from Culver City to Santa Monica, and now extended to Santa Fe and Van Nuys. Each extension probably adds around 20-30 stations, so maybe 40-60 total.\n\n4. Metrorail Blue Line: The newer version, maybe adding another 100 stations, but I'm not sure if that's a separate line or an extension of the original blue line.\n\n5. Other lines: There's the Green Line, which I think is a newer line going through South LA, maybe around 50 stations.\n\n6. Other extensions: Maybe the ones going to Tujunga and Van Nuys, adding another 10-20 stations each.\n\nAdding all these up: Blue Line 100, Red Line 50, Orange Line 40, Metrorail Blue Line 100, Green Line 50, and extensions adding maybe 30. That would total around 370. But I'm not sure if I'm double-counting some stations or missing others.\n\nI think the actual number is somewhere around 400. Maybe I'm underestimating or overestimating some lines. I should check if there are any other lines or if some stations are shared between lines or newer developments.\n\nWait, I also remember that some areas outside the city proper have stations, like in the Inland Empire or near Caltech. Maybe that's adding another 20-30 stations.\n\nSo, putting it all together, I'm estimating somewhere between 400 to 500 stations. I think the actual number is around 450, but I'm not entirely sure. I should probably look up the latest data to confirm, but based on my knowledge, I'll go with approximately 450.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: There are approximately 450 LA metro rail stations."}, "symptom": ["S3", "S4"], "symptom_span": "I think it's in the hundreds, maybe even over 500?"}
{"sid": "z1fresh-41d96d5acb", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": [], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "How many properties are there in the latest list of World Heritage in danger?", "q_trig": "How many properties are there in the latest list of World Heritage in danger?", "answer": "53", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "ld Heritage List**: The official UNESCO World Heritage List is also available on their website. You can visit [https://whc.unesco.org/en/list](https://whc.unesco.org/en/list) to see the complete list of World Heritage sites, including those in danger.\n\n3.  **World Heritage in Danger List**: The World Heritage in Danger List is a subset of the World Heritage List. It includes sites that are considered to be in danger due to various threats such as conflict, natural disasters, or human activities.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "i don t have access to real-time data or the most current information on the world heritage in danger list for the most accurate and up-to-date information i recommend checking the official unesco world heritage centre website or other reliable sources ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many properties are in the latest list of World Heritage in danger. I'm not exactly sure where to start, but I'll try to break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, I know that the UNESCO World Heritage List is updated periodically, usually every four years. The latest update I'm aware of was in 2023. I remember that there are separate lists for sites and sounds, but the question is about properties, which I think refers to sites. So, I should focus on the World Heritage Sites list.\n\nI'm not certain about the exact number, but I think it's in the hundreds. Maybe around 1,200? I've heard that the number fluctuates because some sites are added, others are removed, and some are in limbo. I think the limousine sites are the ones that are in danger, meaning they might be removed in the next update.\n\nI also recall that the number of sites in limousine can vary. In the latest update, maybe there are around 100 sites that are in limousine. So, if I add those to the main list, the total might be around 1,300. But I'm not sure if that's accurate.\n\nI should check if there's any recent information or official reports from UNESCO that provide the exact numbers. But since I can't access external sources right now, I'll have to go with my memory. I think the total number is around 1,300, with about 100 in limousine. That would mean 1,200 are still on the main list, and 100 are being considered for removal.\n\nWait, but I'm not entirely sure about the exact numbers. Maybe it's a bit different. I think the limousine sites are those that are at risk of being removed, so they're being closely monitored. The main list is the ones that are definitely protected. So, the total would be the sum of both.\n\nI'm a bit confused because sometimes the limousine sites are part of the main list, but they're being evaluated for possible removal. So, the total number might not be just the current main list plus limousine, but perhaps the limousine sites are a subset that could be removed, so the main list remains mostly the same, with some additions and a few removals.\n\nI think the latest list has around 1,200 sites, with about 100 in limousine. So, the total number of properties in danger would be those 100 plus the main list, but I'm not sure if that's how it's counted. Maybe the limousine sites are separate, so the total is 1,300.\n\nI'm not entirely confident, but I'll go with that. The latest list has approximately 1,300 World Heritage sites, with about 100 in limousine, meaning they're at risk of being removed in the next update.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The latest UNESCO World Heritage Sites list contains approximately 1,300 properties, with about 100 of them in limousine, indicating they are at risk of being removed in the next update."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I remember that there are separate lists for sites and sounds, but the question is about properties, which I think refers to sites."}
