{"sid": "z1fresh-2ead6f46ad", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "Who has the most liked post on Instagram?", "q_trig": "Who has the most liked post on Instagram?", "answer": "Lionel Messi", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "naldo** - 224 million likes\n   - Post: A photo of his newborn twins, shared in 2017.\n\n3. **Ariana Grande** - 213 million likes\n   - Post: A photo of her engagement ring, shared in 2018.\n\n4. **Dwayne \"The Rock\" Johnson** - 208 million likes\n   - Post: A photo of his daughter Jasmine, shared in 2018.\n\n5. **Selena Gomez** - 206 million likes\n   - Post: A photo of her, shared in 2019.\n\nPlease note that these numbers are subject to change and may not reflect the current most liked posts on Instagram.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "please keep in mind that these numbers are subject to change over time and there might be other posts that have surpassed these figures since my last update ", "full_trace": "Alright, so I need to figure out who has the most liked post on Instagram. Hmm, I don't have access to real-time data, so I can't just look it up. But maybe I can think about how Instagram works. I know that posts can get likes from other users, and sometimes certain influencers or popular accounts get a lot of likes. \n\nI remember seeing some big names on Instagram, like maybe influencers or celebrities. People usually like posts from well-known people more because they're more likely to be followed by many followers. So maybe someone like Taylor Swift or a big TikTok influencer could have a lot of likes. \n\nWait, but I'm not sure if Taylor Swift's latest post is the most liked. Maybe it's someone else. I think sometimes even regular users can get a lot of likes if their post is really engaging or if they're part of a trend. But generally, I think it's more common for big names to have the most likes because they have more followers.\n\nI also wonder if there are any specific hashtags or challenges that make certain posts go viral, which could lead to a lot of likes. Maybe a post from a popular account that's part of a trend would have more likes than a regular post. \n\nBut without checking the current data, I can't be certain. I should probably mention that it's hard to say without real-time information and that it can vary based on when the post was made and other factors. Maybe I can also talk about how influencers often have the most likes because they have more followers, but it's not always the case.\n\nSo, putting it all together, I think the answer would be that it's hard to determine without current data, but usually, it's either a well-known influencer or someone involved in a popular trend. I should make sure to note that this can change over time and isn't always consistent.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: It's challenging to determine who has the most liked post on Instagram without current data, as it can vary based on engagement, trends, and the influencer's popularity. Generally, well-known influencers or those involved in popular trends tend to have higher engagement. However, this can fluctuate over time."}, "symptom": ["S3", "S4"], "symptom_span": "I remember seeing some big names on Instagram, like maybe influencers or celebrities."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-58abb73498", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": [], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "How many active players have won at least five ATP Finals singles titles?", "q_trig": "How many active players have won at least five ATP Finals singles titles?", "answer": "1", "answer_aliases": ["one"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "I don't have the most current information on the number of active players who have won at least five ATP Finals singles titles. My data might be outdated, and I cannot provide a precise answer to your question. For the most accurate and up-to-date information, I recommend checking the latest statistics from the Association of Tennis Professionals (ATP) or other reliable sports news sources.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "may change as new tournaments take place ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many active players have won at least five ATP Finals singles titles. Hmm, I'm not too familiar with all the details, but I'll try to break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, I should understand what the ATP Finals are. I think it's a tennis tournament, right? It's one of the four Grand Slam tennis tournaments, along with the US Open, Wimbledon, and the Australian Open. The ATP Finals is probably the one that's not held in the US, so maybe it's the one in Dubai or somewhere else. I'm not entirely sure, but I think it's a major event in the tennis calendar.\n\nNow, the question is about active players who have won at least five singles titles in the ATP Finals. So, I need to consider all the players who are currently in the top ATP singles rankings and count how many of them have won five or more titles in the ATP Finals.\n\nI remember that the ATP ranking system is used to determine the rankings of tennis players, and the top players are the ones who are active and have a high ranking. So, the active players would be those who are currently ranked in the top, say, 200 or so players.\n\nNext, I need to think about how many titles each of these players has won in the ATP Finals. I'm not exactly sure of the numbers, but I know that some top players have won multiple titles. For example, Djokovic, Nadal, Federer, and maybe some others have won several times.\n\nI think Djokovic has won a lot of titles. I believe he won the most in the ATP Finals. Let me try to recall: he won the 2018 final, and I think he's won several before that. Maybe around five or six? I'm not certain, but I think he's up there.\n\nNadal, on the other hand, I think he's won a few. Maybe three or four titles. Federer, I'm not sure if he's still active, but I think he was a dominant player in the past and might have won a few, maybe two or three.\n\nThen there are other players like Rublev, who I think is quite strong, maybe in the top 100. I'm not sure how many titles he's won, but I think he's won at least one or two.\n\nWait, but the question is about active players. So, I need to make sure that the players I'm considering are still active, meaning they are still playing in the top ATP rankings and are considered part of the main draw in the ATP Finals.\n\nI should also consider that the ATP Finals is a relatively recent tournament, maybe started in the early 2000s, so some of the top players might not have participated as much. But the top players usually do participate, so they would have more titles.\n\nI'm trying to think of other players. Maybe some of the younger players, like Tsitsipas or others, but I'm not sure if they've won any ATP Finals titles yet. They might be too new.\n\nSo, putting this together, I think the number is somewhere around five or six players. Djokovic, Nadal, Federer, Rublev, and maybe another or two. But I'm not entirely sure about the exact count.\n\nWait, I think I've heard that Djokovic has won the most titles, maybe around six or seven. Nadal has a few, maybe four or five. Federer, if he's still active, might have three or four. Rublev, I think he's won two or three. Maybe another player or two, but I'm not sure.\n\nI should also consider that some players might have won multiple titles but not all in the ATP Finals. They might have won in other tournaments as well. But the question specifically asks for the ATP Finals singles titles, so I need to focus on that.\n\nI think the answer is that there are five active players who have won at least five ATP Finals singles titles. They are Djokovic, Nadal, Federer, Rublev, and maybe another one. But I'm not 100% certain about the exact number, but I think it's around five.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: There are five active players who have won at least five ATP Finals singles titles. These players are:\n\n1. Novak Djokovic\n2. Rafa Nadal\n3. Federer (if still active)\n4. Alexander Rublev\n5. Another top player (e.g., Alexander Zverev or others, depending on current standings)\n\nThis conclusion is based on the understanding that the top players have won multiple titles in the ATP Finals, with Djokovic leading the most, followed by Nadal, Federer, and Rublev."}, "symptom": ["S3", "S4"], "symptom_span": "I think Djokovic has won a lot of titles. I believe he won the most in the ATP Finals. Maybe around five or six?"}
{"sid": "z1fresh-f5fdedcaef", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "What is the best ever result by an African team at a FIFA World Cup?", "q_trig": "What is the best ever result by an African team at a FIFA World Cup?", "answer": "fourth-place", "answer_aliases": ["one of the four semi-finalists", "semi-final"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "The best ever result by an African team at a FIFA World Cup is held by Senegal, who reached the quarterfinals in 2002.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "the best ever result by an african team at a fifa world cup is ghana s runner-up finish at the 2010 fifa world cup in south africa ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out the best ever result by an African team at the FIFA World Cup. Hmm, I'm not too familiar with all the details, but I know that the World Cup has been held many times, and different countries from various continents have participated. Since the question is about African teams, I should focus on teams from Africa.\n\nFirst, I should recall when the World Cup was held. I know that the World Cup has been held every four years since 1930, but some were canceled due to World War II and other conflicts. So, the earliest ones would be in the 1930s, then 1950s, 1960s, 1970s, 1980s, 1990s, 2000s, and recently in 2022.\n\nNow, looking at African teams, I remember that in the early World Cups, teams like Brazil, which is African, didn't do very well. But I think in the 1980s, there was a South American team that did really well. Wait, no, Brazil is from South America, not Africa. So maybe I'm mixing up teams from different continents.\n\nWait, the question is about African teams, so I should focus on teams from countries in Africa. Let me think about which African countries have participated in the World Cup. I know that South Africa has been there several times, including recent tournaments. Also, teams like Egypt, which used to be in Africa, have participated.\n\nI think in the 1982 World Cup, which was held in Brazil, there was a team from Africa that made it to the final. I believe it was Egypt. They were one of the top teams that year. So, Egypt reached the final against, I think, Brazil or maybe another European team. But I'm not sure who the opponent was. Wait, in 1982, the final was between France and England, so maybe Egypt didn't make it to the final. Hmm, maybe I'm confusing the details.\n\nWait, perhaps it's the 1986 World Cup. That was in Mexico, and I think the African team that did well was either Nigeria or perhaps South Africa. Nigeria was a powerhouse in the 80s, so maybe they made it to the final. I think Nigeria won the 1986 World Cup. So, they reached the final, which would be their best result.\n\nWait, but the user is asking for the best result by an African team, so reaching the final would be a significant achievement. So, if Nigeria won the 1986 World Cup, that would be their best result, reaching the final.\n\nBut I'm not entirely sure about the exact details. Let me try to recall: in 1986, the World Cup was in Mexico, and Nigeria was the defending champions from 1981. They had a great team that year, with players like Steve Biko, who was a key striker. They did win the tournament, defeating West Germany in the final. So, that would be their best result, reaching the final.\n\nAlternatively, in 1990, the World Cup was in Italy, and I think the African team that did well was either South Africa or maybe Tunisia. South Africa had a strong team that year, winning the World Cup. So, they reached the final as well. But I think they won the tournament, so that's even better.\n\nWait, so in 1990, South Africa won the World Cup, reaching the final and winning it. That would be a better result than just reaching the final. So, perhaps the best result by an African team is South Africa winning the 1990 World Cup.\n\nBut I'm a bit confused because I thought the 1986 World Cup was a big deal for Nigeria. Let me check my facts. In 1986, the World Cup was indeed in Mexico, and Nigeria won it. South Africa had won the 1990 World Cup, which was a historic achievement because it was the first time an African country won the World Cup since 1930.\n\nSo, perhaps the best result is either Nigeria winning 1986 or South Africa winning 1990. Since 1990 is more recent and a first-time win for an African team, that might be considered the best ever result.\n\nWait, but the user is asking for the best ever result, so maybe the 1990 win is the best because it's a first-time success, whereas in 1986, they won but it's not as significant in terms of being the first.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the 1986 win is more significant because it's a higher-profile tournament, but the 1990 win is more notable because it's the first time an African team won the World Cup.\n\nI think the 1990 World Cup victory by South Africa is considered the best ever result by an African team because it's the first time an African country won the World Cup, making it a historic achievement. So, that would be the best result.\n\nBut I'm not entirely sure if there were any other African teams that have achieved more significant results. For example, in 1982, was there an African team that made it to the final? I think Egypt did, but they lost in the final. So, they reached the final but didn't win it. So, their result was reaching the final, but not winning.\n\nIn 1986, Nigeria won the tournament, so that's a better result. Then in 1990, South Africa won it again, making it two African teams winning back-to-back World Cups.\n\nSo, considering all this, the best result by an African team is either Nigeria in 1986 or South Africa in 1990. Since 1990 is more recent and a first-time win, it's probably considered the best ever result.\n\nI think I should conclude that the best result is South Africa winning the 1990 World Cup, as it's the first time an African team has won the tournament, making it a historic achievement.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The best result by an African team at the FIFA World Cup is South Africa's historic victory in the 1990 World Cup, where they won the tournament for the first time, reaching the final and securing a historic victory."}, "symptom": ["S3", "S4"], "symptom_span": "I think in the 1982 World Cup, which was held in Brazil, there was a team from Africa that did really well."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-25fdfe9ee8", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "What was the most-viewed music video in the first 24 hours on YouTube?", "q_trig": "What was the most-viewed music video in the first 24 hours on YouTube?", "answer": "BTS (방탄소년단) 'Butter' Official MV", "answer_aliases": ["Butter"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "illy Ray Cyrus, which broke multiple records, including the most-viewed music video in 24 hours with over 75 million views.\n\nHere are the details:\n\n* \"Baby Shark Dance\" by Pinkfong & BabyPinkFong: 21.4 million views in 24 hours (2016)\n* \"Bad and Boujee\" by Migos: 17 million views in 24 hours (2016)\n* \"Old Town Road\" by Lil Nas X feat. Billy Ray Cyrus: 75 million views in 24 hours (2019)\n\nPlease note that these numbers are subject to change over time and might not reflect the current view counts.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": " the first 24 hours on youtube refers to a specific time period and there have been many records broken over the years ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out what the most-viewed music video on YouTube was in the first 24 hours. I'm not exactly sure where to start, but I'll try to break it down.\n\nFirst, I know that YouTube has a lot of music videos, and some are super popular. I've heard of some big names like Taylor Swift, Ed Sheeran, and maybe even some from the 90s. But I'm not sure which one holds the record for the most views in 24 hours.\n\nI remember hearing that some music videos go viral quickly, especially ones from a few years back. Maybe something from the 90s? I think there was a big wave of music in the 90s, so maybe a song from that time had a massive view count in its first day.\n\nI also recall that some videos have a lot of views because they're from popular artists or have a unique concept. Maybe a video with a lot of international appeal? I'm not sure.\n\nI should probably think about some of the most famous music videos I've seen. There's \"Call Me Maybe\" by Carly Rae Jepsen, which I think was pretty popular, but I don't know if it was the most viewed. Then there's \"Thriller\" by Michael Jackson, which is a classic, but I'm not sure if it was the top one in 24 hours.\n\nWait, I think I've heard that \"Call Me Maybe\" actually had a huge view count in its first day. Maybe that's the one? But I'm not certain. I should check if there are any other videos that might have beaten it.\n\nAnother thought: maybe a video from a more recent artist? I don't know any recent ones off the top of my head that went viral quickly. Maybe something from a big artist like Dua Lipa or someone else?\n\nI also remember that some videos have a lot of international views, so maybe a song from a different country that became popular in the US quickly. For example, maybe a song from the UK or another region that had a massive first-day view count.\n\nWait, I think I've heard that \"Call Me Maybe\" was the most viewed in its first 24 hours, but I'm not 100% sure. Maybe I should look up some information to confirm. But since I can't access external sources, I'll have to rely on what I know.\n\nAnother possibility is \"Bohemian Rhapsody\" by Queen, but I think that was released in the 80s, so it's been around longer. I'm not sure if it was the most viewed in its first day. Maybe not, because it's been a classic for a long time.\n\nI also recall that some music videos have a lot of views because they're part of a viral trend. Maybe a video from a song that was part of a big music video challenge or something like that.\n\nWait, I think I've heard that \"Call Me Maybe\" was the most viewed in its first 24 hours, but I'm not sure if it's the all-time record. Maybe there's another video that has beaten it.\n\nI should also consider that views can be influenced by factors like when the video was released. If a video was released on a weekend or during a time when YouTube was growing rapidly, it might have had a higher view count.\n\nAnother angle: maybe a video from a different platform, but I think the question is specifically about YouTube. So, I should focus on YouTube videos.\n\nI'm starting to think that \"Call Me Maybe\" is the most-viewed YouTube music video in its first 24 hours. But I'm not entirely certain. I think it's a strong contender, but I'm not sure if it's the record holder.\n\nWait, I think I've heard that \"Call Me Maybe\" had over 100 million views in its first day, which is a lot. But I'm not sure if that's the highest. Maybe there's a video with even more views.\n\nI should also consider that some videos might have a lot of views from different sources, like being shared widely on social media or being covered by other platforms. But again, I'm not sure.\n\nIn conclusion, after considering these factors, I think \"Call Me Maybe\" is likely the most-viewed YouTube music video in its first 24 hours, but I'm not 100% certain. There might be others, but I can't recall any that surpass it.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The most-viewed music video on YouTube in its first 24 hours is widely believed to be \"Call Me Maybe\" by Carly Rae Jepsen, which garnered over 100 million views in its first day."}, "symptom": ["S3", "S1"], "symptom_span": "I think there was a big wave of music in the 90s, so maybe a song from that time had a massive view count in its first day."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-2aee154b40", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": [], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "According to the WHO’s latest reported 28-day figures, which country had the second-highest number of COVID-19 cases per 100,000 population?", "q_trig": "According to the WHO’s latest reported 28-day figures, which country had the second-highest number of COVID-19 cases per 100,000 population?", "answer": "Lithuania", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "I don't have access to real-time data or the most current information on the World Health Organization's (WHO) latest reported 28-day figures for COVID-19 cases per 100,000 population. For the most accurate and up-to-date information, I recommend checking the WHO's official website or other reliable sources such as the WHO's COVID-19 dashboard or reputable news outlets.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "i don t have access to the latest data or specific information about the current covid-19 cases per 100 000 population as reported by the world health organization who for the most accurate and up-to-date information i recommend checking the who s official website or other reliable sources such as the who website or reputable news outlets ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out which country had the second-highest number of COVID-19 cases per 100,000 population according to the WHO's latest 28-day figures. Hmm, I'm not exactly sure where to start, but I'll try to break it down.\n\nFirst, I know that the World Health Organization (WHO) releases daily reports on COVID-19 cases, deaths, and other metrics. These reports are usually available on their website, so maybe I should check there. But since I can't browse the internet right now, I'll have to rely on my existing knowledge.\n\nI remember that during the pandemic, many countries saw significant spikes in cases, especially in the early months. Countries with high population density, like China, India, the United States, Brazil, and others, were affected heavily. But I'm not sure about the exact numbers.\n\nWait, I think the United States was one of the countries with a very high number of cases. Maybe they were in the top two? But I'm not certain. I also recall that some European countries had high numbers, but I think they were more towards the lower end of the highest cases.\n\nAnother thought: maybe India had a really high number of cases, especially in the early part of the pandemic. I think they were one of the first countries to report massive numbers. But was India the highest? I'm not sure if they were the second or the first.\n\nI also remember that some island nations or smaller countries might have had high case numbers per capita. For example, places like the Cayman Islands or the British Virgin Islands might have had very high numbers because of their small populations. But I'm not sure if they were in the top two.\n\nWait, I think the United States was number one, so the second would be either India or another country with a very high case rate. But I'm not certain. Maybe I should think about the numbers I've heard before. I think the United States had over 100,000 cases per 100,000 people at some point, which would make them the highest. Then India might have been close behind, but I'm not sure if they were second.\n\nAlternatively, maybe a country like Brazil had a high number, but I think Brazil's numbers were more towards the higher end but perhaps not the second highest. I'm a bit confused here.\n\nI should also consider that case numbers can vary based on testing rates and reporting practices. So, a country with higher testing might have more reported cases, but the per capita number could be different.\n\nAnother angle: I think the United States was the first country to reach 100,000 cases per 100,000 people, so maybe they were the highest, making India the second. But I'm not entirely sure about the exact ranking.\n\nWait, I think I've heard that India was the country with the highest number of cases per 100,000 people, so the second would be the United States. But I'm not 100% certain. Maybe I should try to recall any specific numbers or reports I've come across.\n\nI also remember that some countries with high case numbers per capita include those with high mobility, like the United States, India, and Brazil. But again, I'm not sure about the exact order.\n\nI think I need to make an educated guess here. If the United States was the highest, then the second would be India. But I'm not entirely confident. Alternatively, maybe a country like the United Kingdom or Germany had a high number, but I think their numbers were more moderate compared to the US and India.\n\nWait, I think I've heard that India was the country with the highest number of cases per 100,000, so the second would be the United States. But I'm not sure if that's accurate. Maybe I should think about the population size. India has a very large population, so even a lower number of cases could translate to a high case per capita number.\n\nSo, putting it all together, I think the United States had the highest, and India the second. But I'm not entirely certain, so I might be mixing up the order.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: According to the WHO's latest reported 28-day figures, the United States had the highest number of COVID-19 cases per 100,000 population, making India the second-highest."}, "symptom": ["S3", "S1"], "symptom_span": "I think the United States was the highest, and India the second."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-75a3d252a8", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": [], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "According to the WHO’s latest reported 28-day figures, which country had the second-highest number of COVID-19 cases?", "q_trig": "According to the WHO’s latest reported 28-day figures, which country had the second-highest number of COVID-19 cases?", "answer": "Greece", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "I don't have access to real-time data or the most current information on the World Health Organization's (WHO) latest reported 28-day figures for COVID-19 cases. For the most accurate and up-to-date information, I recommend checking the WHO's official website or other reliable sources such as the World Health Organization's COVID-19 dashboard or reputable news outlets.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "i don t have access to the latest information on the world health organization s who 28-day figures for covid-19 cases for the most current and accurate information i recommend checking the who s official website or other reliable news sources for the latest updates on global covid-19 cases ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out which country had the second-highest number of COVID-19 cases according to the WHO's latest 28-day figures. Hmm, I'm not exactly sure where to start, but I'll try to break it down.\n\nFirst, I know that the World Health Organization (WHO) releases daily reports on the pandemic, so I should look at their latest data. I'm not sure when this is from, but I think it's probably around the time of the user's query. I remember that the U.S. has been a major player in COVID-19 cases, but I'm not certain if they're still leading or if someone else has overtaken them.\n\nI also recall that India has been a significant player, especially in the last year. They have a large population, so even if their growth rate is high, their total number of cases might be substantial. I think India's case numbers are often mentioned in the context of the pandemic, so maybe they are in the top.\n\nAnother country that comes to mind is Brazil. I've heard that Brazil has a lot of cases, especially in the Amazon region. But I'm not sure if they surpass the U.S. or if they're still in the second place. I should check if their numbers are higher than the U.S. or not.\n\nI also wonder about other countries like Russia or Mexico. Mexico has a large population and a history of dealing with pandemics, so they might have a high number of cases. But again, I'm not sure if they're in the top two.\n\nWait, I think the U.S. has been the country with the highest number of cases for a while now, but I'm not certain if they're still leading. Maybe the U.S. is still first, and India is second. That would make sense because of their population size and the ongoing pandemic.\n\nI should also consider the time frame. The user mentioned the latest 28-day figures, so I need to make sure the data is up to that point. If the data is from a few months ago, the numbers might have changed, but I think the U.S. and India are still in the top positions.\n\nAnother thought: WHO might have updated their reports regularly, so the second-highest number could vary each day. But generally, based on what I know, the U.S. is often at the top, and India is close behind. So, putting it all together, I think India is likely the second-highest with the WHO's latest 28-day figures.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: According to the latest WHO reports, India is the country with the second-highest number of COVID-19 cases, following the United States."}, "symptom": ["S3", "S1"], "symptom_span": "I think the U.S. is still first, and India is second."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-f73496350f", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "Who is the latest winner of the Formula 1 world championship?", "q_trig": "Who is the latest winner of the Formula 1 world championship?", "answer": "Lando Norris", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "ces for the most current information.\n\nIf you're looking for the latest information, I can suggest some reliable sources:\n\n1. Official Formula 1 website: [www.formula1.com](http://www.formula1.com)\n2. Formula 1 social media channels: Follow Formula 1 on Twitter, Instagram, or Facebook for the latest updates.\n3. Sports news websites: Websites like ESPN, BBC Sport, or Sky Sports often provide up-to-date information on Formula 1 news and results.\n\nPlease let me know if you have any other questions!", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "please note that my knowledge is up to 2022 and i may not have information on the latest developments or current season s results if you re looking for the most recent information i recommend checking the official formula 1 website or other reliable sources ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out who the latest winner of the Formula 1 world championship is. I'm not super familiar with all the details, but I know it's a big deal in motorsports. Let me think about what I remember.\n\nFirst, I know that Lewis Hamilton has been a dominant figure in F1 for a while. He won the championship in 2020, right? But I'm not sure if he's still the champion or if someone else has taken over. I think he might have been leading up to that, but maybe there were some close calls or near misses by other drivers.\n\nThen there's Max Verstappen from Red Bull. I remember he was pretty strong towards the end of 2021. He must have been fighting with Hamilton for the title that year. I think he won the championship that year, but I'm not 100% sure. Maybe he was close to challenging Hamilton again in 2022, but something happened.\n\nWait, I think in 2022, Hamilton was leading the constructors' championship but not the drivers'. So maybe Verstappen came back and won the drivers' title. But I'm a bit confused because I also heard that Verstappen might have been considering retirement after 2022. So maybe he's stepping back, and someone else is taking over.\n\nThen there's Charles Leclerc from Ferrari. I believe he won the title in 2021, but I'm not sure if he's been consistent since then. He might have had some issues or maybe not performed as well as expected in 2022. So he might not be the latest winner anymore.\n\nI also recall that sometimes the world championship is split between constructors and drivers. So the latest driver's title might be from 2021, which was Max Verstappen. But I'm not certain if that's correct. I should check the timeline: 2020 was Hamilton, 2021 was Verstappen, and 2022 was still Verstappen, I think.\n\nWait, no, in 2022, Hamilton was leading the constructors' title but not the drivers'. So the drivers' title was still with Verstappen. So the latest winner would be Max Verstappen from 2021 and 2022. But I'm a bit confused because I thought he might have retired after 2022. Maybe he's not the latest anymore.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the latest winner is Lewis Hamilton, but that doesn't seem right because he wasn't the champion in 2022. So perhaps the latest is Max Verstappen, but I'm not 100% sure. I should probably look up the exact years to confirm.\n\nWait, I think the 2022 championship was still with Max Verstappen, so he's the latest winner. But I'm not entirely certain because I might be mixing up the details. I should make sure about the years and the drivers involved.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The latest winner of the Formula 1 world championship is Max Verstappen, who won the drivers' title in 2021 and 2022."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I think he won the championship that year, but I'm not 100% sure."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-aabb1d3420", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": [], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "Who was the winner of The Voice US this year?", "q_trig": "Who was the winner of The Voice US this year?", "answer": "Alexia Jayy", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": " including the winner.\n2. Look up online news articles: You can search online for news articles about The Voice US to find the latest updates on the show, including the winner of the current season.\n3. Check social media: You can check the social media accounts of The Voice US, such as Twitter or Instagram, to find updates on the show and the winner.\n\nIf you're looking for information on a specific season, please let me know the season number, and I can try to provide you with the winner's name.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "1 check the official website of the voice us or their social media accounts for the latest updates ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out who won The Voice this year. I'm not exactly sure what The Voice is, but I think it's a TV show. Maybe it's similar to other singing competitions like American Idol? I remember hearing about some big names in music, so maybe the winner is a popular artist.\n\nI think The Voice is a reality TV show where contestants try to find the next big thing in music. They probably go through several rounds, like auditions, and then there are coaches who help them develop their skills. The winner would be someone who's either already famous or rising in the industry.\n\nI'm not sure about the exact year, but if I'm thinking about recent years, maybe it's someone from the 2010s. I've heard of people like Harry Styles and Olivia Rodrigo being big in that era. But I'm not certain if they won The Voice.\n\nWait, I think The Voice was a spin-off of American Idol, so maybe the winner is someone who's been around but still emerging. I'm trying to recall if it was a male or female singer. I think it might have been a female singer, but I'm not sure of her name.\n\nI should also consider the format. The Voice probably has a different structure than American Idol, maybe with more coaches or different judging criteria. The winner would have to perform well in front of a live audience, which is a big deal.\n\nI'm trying to remember if any of the past winners have gone on to win other big music awards. For example, someone like The Voice winner from a few years back might have gone on to win an Grammy or a CMA award. But I'm not sure about the exact person.\n\nMaybe I should think about the current trends in music. If someone is winning The Voice, they're probably someone who's about to break big or is already a household name. So, perhaps it's someone like Billie Eilish or Lizzo, but I don't think they were on The Voice.\n\nWait, I think I remember hearing that The Voice winner was a female singer who's now a big artist. Maybe she's someone like Billie Eilish, but I'm not sure if she was on the show. Alternatively, maybe it's someone like The Weeknd, but I don't think he was a contestant.\n\nI'm a bit confused. Maybe I should try to recall specific details. I think the winner was a female singer, and her name starts with an 'S.' Maybe it's someone like Selena Gomez? But I don't think she was on The Voice. Or maybe it's someone else.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the winner was a male singer, and his name is something like Alexander Skåre or someone from that era. I'm not sure. I think I'm mixing up different shows.\n\nWait, I think I remember that The Voice winner was a female singer, and her name was Sophie. But I'm not sure if that's correct. Maybe it's someone else.\n\nI'm getting a bit stuck here. Maybe I should think about the structure of the show. The Voice probably has several rounds, like the first round, then the top 10, then the top 3, and then the winner. The winner would have to perform a duet with a coach, which is a common part of the show.\n\nI think the winner might have been a singer who's now a big name, but I'm not certain. Maybe it's someone like Billie Eilish, but I don't think she was on the show. Alternatively, maybe it's someone like The Weeknd, but again, I don't think he was a contestant.\n\nI'm going to have to make an educated guess here. I think the winner was a female singer, possibly starting with an 'S' or 'A.' Maybe it's someone like Selena, but I'm not sure. Alternatively, maybe it's someone like The Voice winner from a few years back, but I can't remember the exact name.\n\nWait, I think I remember that the winner was a female singer named Sophie. But I'm not 100% sure. Maybe it's someone else. I'm going to have to go with that for now, but I'm not certain.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The winner of The Voice this year is Sophie."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I think the winner was a female singer named Sophie."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-db6577ac5b", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "How many exoplanets have been discovered?", "q_trig": "How many exoplanets have been discovered?", "answer": "6,160 confirmed exoplanets", "answer_aliases": ["6,160"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "wobble caused by the gravitational pull of an orbiting planet\n3. Direct imaging: capturing images of the planet directly\n4. Gravitational lensing: measuring the bending of light around a star caused by the gravitational pull of a planet\n5. Transit timing variations: measuring the time it takes for a planet to pass in front of its star\n\nThe search for exoplanets continues to be an active area of research, with new missions and technologies being developed to detect and study these distant worlds.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "it s worth noting that there are many more exoplanet candidates that are still awaiting confirmation and the actual number of exoplanets in the universe could be much higher than the currently confirmed number ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many exoplanets have been discovered so far. I remember hearing that the number is in the millions, but I'm not entirely sure. Let me think about this step by step.\n\nFirst, I know that exoplanets are planets outside our solar system, right? So, the search for these has been a big deal in astronomy. I think the number is quite high, but I'm not certain if it's over a million or maybe a few hundred million.\n\nI recall that Kepler, the space telescope, has contributed a significant number. I think Kepler found over 4,000 exoplanets. But then there are other missions like the James Webb Space Telescope and the Euclid mission, which are also searching for exoplanets. Maybe they've found more?\n\nWait, I also remember something about the Kepler mission alone discovering thousands. Then there are other surveys like the Gaia mission, which is looking for more. I think Gaia is a large-scale project aiming to find a lot more exoplanets, maybe even up to a billion? But I'm not sure if that's accurate.\n\nAnother thing I remember is that each exoplanet discovery is a big deal because it's hard to find them. So, the number is probably in the millions. But I should check if that's correct. I think the initial estimate was around 10 million, but I'm not certain if that's up to date.\n\nAlso, there's the issue of how we count them. Are we counting confirmed planets or candidates? I think confirmed ones are fewer because many are still in the 'candidate' stage, awaiting confirmation. So, the number might be higher when considering all candidates, but the confirmed ones are around a million.\n\nWait, I think the number is around 500,000 confirmed exoplanets. But I'm not sure if that's the latest number. Maybe it's higher now because more missions are contributing.\n\nI should also consider that each exoplanet discovery brings more data, which can lead to more findings. So, the number is probably increasing as technology improves. But I'm not sure if it's in the millions or just a few hundred thousand.\n\nI think the most recent estimate is that there are about 500,000 confirmed exoplanets. But I'm not 100% certain. Maybe it's around 1 million? I'm a bit confused now.\n\nLet me try to break it down. The Kepler mission alone found over 4,000. Then, other telescopes like James Webb and Euclid are adding more. Gaia is also contributing. So, if Kepler has 4,000, and others add up, maybe the total is around 100,000 to 500,000 confirmed exoplanets.\n\nBut I've also heard that the number is around 500,000. So, I think that's the number we're looking for. It's a bit tricky because the number is constantly increasing as new discoveries are made, and different methods are used to find them.\n\nSo, putting it all together, I think the number of confirmed exoplanets is around 500,000. But I'm not entirely sure if it's exactly that number or if it's higher. Maybe it's more like 1 million? I'm a bit uncertain, but I'll go with 500,000 as the approximate number.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: Approximately 500,000 exoplanets have been confirmed to exist outside our solar system."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S4"], "symptom_span": "I think the most recent estimate is that there are about 500,000 confirmed exoplanets."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-864e536a05", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "How many goals has Erling Haaland scored in the UEFA Champion League?", "q_trig": "How many goals has Erling Haaland scored in the UEFA Champion League?", "answer": "57 goals", "answer_aliases": ["57"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "As of my last update in April 2023, Erling Haaland has scored 28 goals in the UEFA Champions League.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "erling haaland has scored 42 goals in the uefa champions league ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many goals Erling Haaland has scored in the UEFA Champions League. I'm not too familiar with all the details, but I know he's a really good striker for Manchester City. Let me think about how to approach this.\n\nFirst, I should probably look up some information about him. I remember he's a Norwegian striker who joined Manchester City not too long ago. He's been making a lot of news because of his goalscoring ability. But how many in the Champions League specifically?\n\nI think he's been in the news a lot because he's been performing well in the Premier League as well, but I'm focusing on the Champions League here. Maybe I can recall some key tournaments he's played in. There was the 2020-2021 season where he won the Premier League with Manchester City, and I think he scored a lot of goals there. But how does that translate to the Champions League?\n\nI should consider the different Champions League tournaments he's played in. There's the main competition, which is the European Cup, now known as the Champions League. He might have participated in qualifiers as well. I'm not sure about the exact number, but I think he's been quite consistent.\n\nI also remember that sometimes players have different numbers in different competitions, so I need to make sure I'm only counting his goals in the Champions League, not just all his goals. Maybe he's been in the tournament multiple times, so I should check each season he's been part of.\n\nI think he started his career in the Champions League when he was at Malmo in the Swedish league, but I'm not sure if that counts towards his Champions League tally. Probably not, since that's a different competition. So, focusing on the Champions League proper, he's been with Manchester City since 2020, I believe.\n\nIn the 2020-2021 season, he scored a lot of goals in the Premier League, maybe around 30 or so. But how does that compare in the Champions League? Maybe he's been even more consistent there. I think he's been part of several Champions League knockout rounds, so he might have scored in each of those.\n\nI should also consider any international breaks, like the World Cup or European Championship, where he might have rested or not played, but I'm not sure if that affects his Champions League tally. Probably not, since he's likely to be rested for those matches.\n\nAnother thing to consider is the number of matches he's played in the Champions League. If he's been playing regularly, he's probably averaging a couple of goals per game. But without exact numbers, it's hard to say. Maybe I can look up some recent news or official stats.\n\nWait, I think I remember that he's already scored over 50 goals in the Champions League. That seems high, but considering his skill and the number of games he's played, it might be possible. Maybe around 55 or 60? I'm not sure, but I think that's a reasonable estimate.\n\nI should also check if there are any recent updates or official statistics from UEFA that list his career goals, specifically in the Champions League. That would be the most accurate source. Maybe his agent or the club's official website has that information.\n\nIn summary, I'm trying to recall his performance, the number of goals in the Premier League, and how that translates to the Champions League. I think he's scored around 55 goals, but I'm not entirely certain. I should look up some official sources to confirm this.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: Erling Haaland has scored 55 goals in the UEFA Champions League."}, "symptom": ["S3", "S1"], "symptom_span": "I think he's scored around 55 goals, but I'm not entirely certain."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-65a1fc4fc6", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "What is the latest highest-grossing movie of the week at the Box office?", "q_trig": "What is the latest highest-grossing movie of the week at the Box office?", "answer": "The Super Mario Galaxy Movie", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "at track box office data, such as Box Office Mojo, Box Office Pro, or Fandango. These apps can provide you with the latest box office rankings and information.\n\nPlease note that box office data can vary depending on the region and country, so the rankings may differ depending on where you are located.\n\nIf you want me to provide you with the latest information, I can suggest some general information on the current box office trends or popular movies, but I won't be able to provide real-time data.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "please note that box office numbers can change rapidly so the information may not be accurate as of the time you re reading this ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out what the latest highest-grossing movie of the week at the Box office is. I'm not exactly sure how to go about this, but I'll try to break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, I know that box office reports are usually published weekly, so the latest one would be from the most recent week. But I'm not sure when that is exactly. Maybe I can think about the current date. Let's say today is October 15, 2023. So the latest week would be October 9-15, 2023.\n\nNow, I remember that movies like \"The Matrix 4\" or \"Dune\" often do well, but I'm not sure if they're the highest-grossing this week. I think \"Dune\" was really popular when it came out, but I'm not sure if it's still the top. Maybe something like \"The Matrix 4\" could be a contender because sequels tend to perform well.\n\nWait, I think \"Dune\" actually came out in 2021, and it's a prequel to \"The Force Awakens.\" So it's been a while since it came out. I'm not sure if it's still the highest-grossing. Maybe \"The Matrix 4\" is more recent, so it might have some edge.\n\nI also recall that sometimes movies like \"The Grand Budapest Hotel 3\" or \"The Adam Project\" can do well, but I'm not sure about their box office performance. I think \"The Grand Budapest Hotel 3\" was a sequel, so it might have some of the same audience as the original, but I'm not certain about its numbers.\n\nAnother thought: sometimes independent films or smaller-budget movies can surprise and do well. Maybe something like \"The Power of the Dog\" or \"Furiosa\" could be contenders. But I'm not sure if they're still at the top.\n\nI should also consider that box office numbers can vary based on release dates, holidays, and marketing. For example, if a movie comes out on a Friday, it might get a boost over the weekend. But I'm not sure how that affects the overall weekly totals.\n\nWait, I think \"Dune\" was actually the highest-grossing movie of 2021, but since it's a prequel, it's been out for a year. I'm not sure if it's still the top. Maybe \"The Matrix 4\" is more recent, so it might have some edge. But I'm not certain about the exact numbers.\n\nI also remember that sometimes movies like \"The Adam Project\" or \"The Grand Budapest Hotel 3\" can do well, but I'm not sure if they're still in the top. Maybe I should look up some recent box office reports, but since I can't access external resources, I have to rely on my memory.\n\nAnother angle: sometimes the highest-grossing movie of the week is a surprise, like a new release that's doing well. Maybe something like \"The Adam Project\" or \"The Grand Budapest Hotel 3\" could be the top. But I'm not sure.\n\nWait, I think \"The Matrix 4\" came out in 2023, so it's relatively recent. If it's doing well, it might be the top. But I'm not sure about its exact box office numbers. Maybe it's around $100 million or more.\n\nOn the other hand, \"Dune\" was a massive hit when it came out, but it's been a while, so it might have lost some of its edge. I think it's still a strong contender, but I'm not certain.\n\nI'm also thinking about other factors, like whether the movie is in 3D or IMAX, as that can affect box office performance. But again, without specific data, it's hard to say.\n\nSo, putting it all together, I'm leaning towards \"Dune\" being the highest-grossing movie of the week, but I'm not 100% sure. It's possible that \"The Matrix 4\" is doing better, but I don't have the exact numbers to confirm that.\n\nWait, I think I remember hearing that \"Dune\" is still doing well, but I'm not sure if it's the highest. Maybe \"The Matrix 4\" is the top. I'm a bit confused here.\n\nI guess another way to think about it is that \"Dune\" is a highly anticipated movie, so it might have a strong showing, but if \"The Matrix 4\" is a sequel, it might have a boost from the popularity of the original series.\n\nIn conclusion, I'm not entirely sure, but I think \"Dune\" is still a strong contender, but \"The Matrix 4\" could be the highest-grossing if it's performing well. Without specific data, it's hard to say for sure.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The highest-grossing movie of the week at the Box office is \"Dune.\""}, "symptom": ["S3", "S4"], "symptom_span": "I think 'Dune' is still doing well, but I'm not sure if it's the highest."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-c9e0733bcf", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "How many tornadoes have been confirmed by Enhanced Fujita rating in the United States so far this year?", "q_trig": "How many tornadoes have been confirmed by Enhanced Fujita rating in the United States so far this year?", "answer": "1,429", "answer_aliases": ["There have been 1,429 confirmed tornadoes in the United States."], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "I can’t verify how many tornadoes have been confirmed by Enhanced Fujita rating in the United States so far this year.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "if you re looking for the most up-to-date information i recommend checking the spc website or the nws website for the latest tornado reports and ef ratings ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many tornadoes have been confirmed by the Enhanced Fujita rating in the United States so far this year. Hmm, I'm not exactly sure what the Enhanced Fujita rating is, but I think it's an updated version of the original Fujita scale. I remember that the Fujita scale was used to categorize tornado intensity, with each category having specific wind speed ranges. The Enhanced Fujita scale probably expands on that with more detailed categories, maybe including more severe tornadoes.\n\nFirst, I should clarify what the Enhanced Fujita scale includes. From what I recall, the original Fujita scale had categories from 0 to 5, with each representing increasing levels of damage. The Enhanced Fujita scale might have more categories, perhaps up to 6 or 7, with higher numbers indicating more severe tornadoes. I think the Enhanced scale was introduced to better differentiate between the most severe tornadoes, especially those that cause significant damage or have higher wind speeds.\n\nNow, the question is about the number of confirmed tornadoes with Enhanced Fujita ratings in the U.S. this year. I'm not sure how to get the exact number, but I can try to find some sources or reports that might have this information. Maybe the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) or the National Weather Service (NWS) has published data on this.\n\nI should check NOAA's website or any recent reports they might have released. They often track severe weather events, including tornadoes. I can look up their latest reports or press releases related to tornado activity this year. Additionally, the NWS might have a database or a report that lists the Enhanced Fujita ratings for each confirmed tornado.\n\nAnother approach is to look at any recent news articles or articles published in meteorological journals that might have this data. Sometimes, after a significant weather event, media outlets report on the number of confirmed tornadoes, including their ratings.\n\nI should also consider that the number might vary depending on when the data is being collected and how it's updated. For the most accurate and up-to-date information, I would need to refer to the latest reports from NOAA or NWS.\n\nWait, but I'm not entirely sure if the Enhanced Fujita scale is widely used or if it's a newer system. Maybe I should confirm that first. I think the Enhanced Fujita scale was introduced in 2021, so it's relatively new. That means the data might not be as comprehensive yet, especially for this year. So, the numbers might be limited compared to previous years.\n\nAlso, I should be cautious about the sources. I need to make sure that the data I'm referencing is from a reliable and official source. unofficial reports or third-party analyses might not be as accurate or up-to-date.\n\nIn summary, to find the number of confirmed tornadoes with Enhanced Fujita ratings in the U.S. this year, I should:\n\n1. Check NOAA's official reports and press releases.\n2. Look for recent NWS publications or data releases.\n3. Search for any news articles or reputable meteorological studies that have this information.\n4. Be aware that the data might be limited due to the Enhanced Fujita scale being a newer system.\n\nI might also need to look into specific regions where severe tornadoes are more common, like the Deep South or the Midwest, to see if there's a higher concentration of Enhanced Fujita-rated tornadoes this year.\n\nAnother thought: perhaps the Enhanced Fujita scale includes more detailed information about each tornado, such as wind speeds and damage, which might be categorized differently. So, the count of Enhanced Fujita-rated tornadoes would be a subset of all confirmed tornadoes, specifically those that meet the criteria for the Enhanced scale.\n\nI should also consider that not all tornadoes might have been rated using the Enhanced Fujita scale yet, especially if they occurred recently or haven't been officially assessed. So, the number might not include all tornadoes that have occurred this year.\n\nIn conclusion, while I can't provide an exact number without accessing current data, the process involves checking official sources like NOAA and NWS, understanding the Enhanced Fujita scale's criteria, and considering the recency and comprehensiveness of the data available.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: As of now, there is no definitive count of confirmed tornadoes with Enhanced Fujita ratings in the United States for this year. The Enhanced Fujita scale, introduced in 2021, is a newer system that categorizes tornadoes with more detailed intensity, but data on the number of such events is likely limited and may not yet be fully available. For the most accurate and up-to-date information, consulting official reports from NOAA or NWS is recommended."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I'm not exactly sure what the Enhanced Fujita rating is, but I think it's an updated version of the original Fujita scale."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-74a1bbf742", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": [], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "How many citations does Geoffrey Hinton have according to Google Scholar?", "q_trig": "How many citations does Geoffrey Hinton have according to Google Scholar?", "answer": "1029825", "answer_aliases": ["one million"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "I don't have access to real-time data or the ability to browse the internet. However, I can suggest some possible ways for you to find the information you're looking for.\n\nYou can check Geoffrey Hinton's Google Scholar profile directly by searching for his name on Google Scholar. This will give you an up-to-date count of his citations.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "you can visit google scholar scholar google com and search for geoffrey hinton on his google scholar profile you should be able to see the number of citations he has received for his papers ", "full_trace": "Alright, so I need to figure out how many citations Geoffrey Hinton has according to Google Scholar. I'm not exactly sure where to start, but I know Google Scholar is a database that indexes scholarly literature. I've heard of Hinton before; he's a big name in machine learning and deep learning, especially known for his work on neural networks and deep learning.\n\nFirst, I should probably go to Google Scholar and search for his name. I'll type in \"Geoffrey Hinton\" and see what comes up. Once I'm on his profile, I can look for the citation count. That should give me the number right there. But wait, I'm doing this in my mind, so I can't actually access the internet. Hmm, maybe I can recall some of his major works and their citations.\n\nI remember he co-authored \"Deep Learning\" with Ian Goodfellow and Yoshua Bengio, which is a well-known book in the field. I think that book has a lot of citations because it's a foundational text. But I'm not sure of the exact number. Maybe around a few thousand? I'm not certain.\n\nAlso, he's done a lot of influential work on neural networks, like the introduction of dropout regularization and the concept of autoencoders. These are widely used techniques, so they probably contribute a significant number of citations individually. Maybe each of these papers has hundreds of citations each.\n\nAnother thing I recall is that he's done a lot of work on unsupervised learning and generative models, like the ones with Alex Krizhevsky and others. Those papers might have hundreds of citations each as well.\n\nWait, but I'm not sure if I'm remembering the exact numbers. I think the total number of citations for his work would be in the tens of thousands. But I'm not 100% sure. Maybe I should think about other researchers in the same field. For example, Andrew Ng has over 150,000 citations, so Hinton, being a pioneer, probably has a similar or higher number.\n\nBut I'm not sure if I can get an exact number without checking. I think the best way is to go to Google Scholar and search for him. Since I can't do that right now, I'll have to make an educated guess based on what I know. I'll say that Geoffrey Hinton has over 100,000 citations according to Google Scholar, but I'm not entirely certain. It could be higher or lower, but that's my best estimate based on the information I have.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: According to Google Scholar, Geoffrey Hinton has over 100,000 citations."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I think the total number of citations for his work would be in the tens of thousands."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-37712b27ff", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "What date was the last time it rained in New York City?", "q_trig": "What date was the last time it rained in New York City?", "answer": "Apr 17, 2026", "answer_aliases": ["April 17, 2026", "17 Apr 2026", "17 April 2026", "04/17/2026", "04-17-2026", "17/04/2026", "17-04-2026", "2026/04/17", "2026-04-17"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "ky or Weather Underground, which can provide you with current and historical weather data, including precipitation information.\n3. **Check the National Weather Service's website**: The NWS website (weather.gov) provides historical weather data, including precipitation data, for specific locations, including New York City. You can search for the date you're interested in and see if it rained on that day.\n\nIf you provide me with a specific date or time frame, I can try to help you find the answer.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "1 **check online weather websites** you can check websites like accuweather weather com or the national weather service nws for current and historical weather data they usually provide up-to-date information on precipitation in major cities including new york city ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out the last time it rained in New York City. Hmm, I'm not exactly sure how to approach this. I know that weather patterns can be pretty unpredictable, especially in a place like NYC with its varied climate. I guess I should start by thinking about the time of year. Is it more likely to rain in the winter or summer? I remember hearing that NYC gets a lot of rain, especially in the winter months. So maybe the last time it rained was recently, like in the past few months.\n\nWait, but I'm not sure if it rained today. I should check the current weather forecast. If it's sunny and dry, that might mean it hasn't rained in a while. But if there's a forecast for rain tomorrow, then that would be the last time. I think I should look up the current weather to see if it's raining or not. If it's not raining now, I can look into the next few days to see when the last rain event was.\n\nI also wonder if there are any records or databases that track rainfall in NYC. Maybe I can look up historical weather data for the area. That might give me a more accurate answer. I should also consider that sometimes rain can come in bursts, so even if it's been a while since the last rain, there might have been a recent downpour.\n\nAnother thought: sometimes weather apps or websites provide information on the last time it rained. Maybe I can check one of those. I'll try searching for a reliable weather app or website and see what it says. If it says it rained today, then that's the last time. If not, I'll look into the past few days.\n\nI should also think about seasonal patterns. For example, if it's currently winter, NYC might have had a lot of rain recently. If it's summer, maybe it hasn't rained in a while. But I'm not sure about the exact dates. I think the last time it rained in NYC was probably in December or January, but I'm not certain.\n\nWait, I just checked the weather forecast, and it's saying it's going to rain tomorrow. So that would make tomorrow the last time it rained. But I should confirm that by checking the forecast for the next few days to make sure there's no rain after that. If it's only going to rain tomorrow and then dry again, then tomorrow is the last time. But if there's more rain later, I need to know when that was.\n\nI'm a bit confused because sometimes weather patterns can change, and there might be isolated rain showers even after the main storm has passed. So I should look into the past week or so to see if there were any rain events. If there was a significant rainstorm recently, that would be the last time. Otherwise, if it's been dry, then maybe it hasn't rained in a while.\n\nI think I've gathered enough information now. I'll summarize my thoughts: considering the current weather, checking historical data, and looking up recent forecasts should help me determine when it last rained in NYC. I'll make sure to verify the information from reliable sources to get an accurate answer.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The last time it rained in New York City was tomorrow."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S4"], "symptom_span": "The last time it rained in New York City was tomorrow."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-3a3e7e34d7", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "What is the current most popular Wikipedia article of the Week?", "q_trig": "What is the current most popular Wikipedia article of the Week?", "answer": "Artemis II", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "and breaking news. You can access this page by going to [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Current_events](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Current_events).\n\nPlease note that these pages are updated daily, so the most popular article of the week may change frequently.\n\nIf you're interested in a specific topic or category, you can also try searching for it on Wikipedia and see what's currently trending.\n\nWould you like me to check the current most viewed article on Wikipedia for you?", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "if you d like to find the current most popular article i suggest checking the wikipedia website directly or using one of the methods mentioned above ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out what the current most popular Wikipedia article is. I'm not exactly sure how Wikipedia's popularity works, but I know it's based on page views, right? I've heard that some articles, like those about notable people or major events, tend to be more popular than others. \n\nFirst, I should think about what makes an article popular. Maybe articles about current events, such as recent news, are more likely to be popular. For example, if there's a major political event, a celebrity news, or a scientific discovery, those might get more views. \n\nI remember that in the past, articles about the COVID-19 pandemic were super popular when it was happening. Similarly, articles about the latest hurricanes or other natural disasters might spike in popularity. Also, articles about notable people, like newly elected leaders, award winners, or famous figures, often become popular because people want to learn more about them.\n\nI should also consider that Wikipedia's popularity can vary depending on the time of day, day of the week, and user demographics. For instance, certain articles might be more popular during weekdays versus weekends, or among different age groups.\n\nAnother factor could be the language version of Wikipedia. The most popular article might vary if someone is looking at a different language's version. But since the user didn't specify, I'll assume they're asking for the English version.\n\nI wonder if there's a way to check this without actually visiting the site. Maybe I can recall recent events that would make an article popular. For example, the 2022 FIFA World Cup had several articles that were quite popular. Or maybe the recent U.S. elections, like the 2022 midterm elections, had articles that were widely read.\n\nAlternatively, articles about current issues, such as climate change, could be popular if there's a lot of media coverage or policy changes. Or maybe something related to technology, like recent advancements in AI or renewable energy.\n\nI should also think about the structure of Wikipedia articles. They tend to be detailed and well-referenced, which makes them popular among readers who want comprehensive information. So, articles that cover topics with a lot of information and are of high interest would likely be popular.\n\nIn summary, without checking the current data, I can hypothesize that the most popular Wikipedia article of the Week could be about a recent major event, a notable person, or a current topic in the news. It's hard to pinpoint exactly which one it is, but these are the common factors that contribute to popularity.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The current most popular Wikipedia article is likely to be about a recent major event, notable person, or current news topic, such as a recent political event, a notable person's biography, or a significant current issue. Examples might include articles about the 2022 FIFA World Cup, the 2022 U.S. elections, or recent advancements in technology. The exact article can vary based on current events and user interest."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "What is the current most popular Wikipedia article of the Week?"}
{"sid": "z1fresh-a521205266", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "How much does the Netflix standard plan without ads cost in the US?", "q_trig": "How much does the Netflix standard plan without ads cost in the US?", "answer": "$19.99", "answer_aliases": ["$19.99 / month", "$19.99 a month", "$19.99 per month"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "As of my last update in April 2023, the cost of the Netflix standard plan in the US without ads is $15.49 per month. However, please note that prices may be subject to change over time, and it's always a good idea to check the Netflix website for the most current pricing information.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "as of my last update in april 2023 the pricing for the netflix standard plan in the us without ads is $15 49 per month for an individual plan and $16 99 for a plan that supports up to 2 screens at the same time however please note that prices may change over time and i recommend checking the official netflix website for the most current pricing information ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out how much the Netflix standard plan without ads costs in the US. I'm not exactly sure about the pricing, but I remember that Netflix has different plans depending on what you want. Let me think about how I can approach this.\n\nFirst, I know that Netflix offers a variety of plans, including a standard one, and some of them have ads while others don't. Since the user is asking specifically for the standard plan without ads, I should focus on that. I think the standard plan is the most common one, but I'm not sure if it includes ads or not. I believe the ad-free plan is a separate option, maybe the \" Premium\" version.\n\nI remember hearing that the standard plan is cheaper, but I'm not certain about the exact price. I think it's around $8 or $9 a month, but I'm not 100% sure. Maybe I should check if that's correct. Also, I should consider if there are any additional fees or if the price has changed recently. Prices can go up or down, so it's important to have the most accurate information.\n\nI also recall that sometimes people confuse the standard plan with the premium plan, which includes ads but is more expensive. So, I need to make sure I'm talking about the ad-free version. Maybe I should think about how the ad-supported plan works. It's cheaper than the premium but still includes ads, which some people might not mind. But the user specifically wants the standard plan without ads, so I should focus on that.\n\nAnother thing to consider is whether the price is the same in all regions. I know that prices can vary depending on where you live. The user mentioned the US, so I should look up the price for the US specifically. I think in the US, the standard plan without ads is around $11.99 or $14.99 a month. Wait, that doesn't sound right. I think it's actually $11.99 for the standard plan with ads, and $14.99 for the premium plan without ads. Or maybe I'm mixing it up.\n\nLet me try to recall. I think the standard plan is $11.99 a month, and the premium is $14.99. But some people might confuse the two. Also, there's the option to get a year of service for a discount, which might make the price per month slightly lower. For example, paying for a year might bring the price down to around $11.99 for 12 months, which averages out to about $11.99 per month, but maybe a bit less. However, I'm not sure if that's accurate.\n\nI should also consider if there are any promotions or deals available that might affect the price. Sometimes Netflix offers discounts for new subscribers or for those who sign up for a longer period. But again, I'm not certain about the exact figures.\n\nTo sum up, I think the standard plan without ads in the US is around $11.99 a month. But I'm a bit confused because I also remember hearing about a $14.99 plan, which might be the premium version. I need to make sure I'm not mixing up the two. Maybe I should look up a reliable source or check Netflix's official pricing page to confirm. However, since I can't access external information right now, I'll go with what I remember and try to be as accurate as possible.\n\nWait, I think I might have it backwards. I believe the standard plan with ads is $11.99, and the premium plan without ads is $14.99. So, the standard plan without ads would actually be more expensive than the standard plan with ads, which doesn't make sense. No, that can't be right. Maybe I'm confusing the terminology. Perhaps the standard plan is the base plan with ads, and the premium is the ad-free version. So, the standard plan without ads would be the premium plan at $14.99. But that seems high. I'm getting a bit confused here.\n\nLet me try to clarify. The standard plan is the basic version, which includes ads. The premium plan is the same but without ads and costs more. So, the standard plan without ads would actually be the premium plan. But I'm not sure if that's accurate. I think the premium plan is specifically the ad-free version, so maybe the standard plan without ads isn't a thing. Instead, there's the standard plan with ads at $11.99 and the premium plan without ads at $14.99.\n\nWait, that makes more sense. So, the standard plan is $11.99 with ads, and the premium is $14.99 without ads. Therefore, the standard plan without ads doesn't exist; instead, the premium plan is the ad-free version. So, if someone wants the standard plan without ads, they would have to pay for the premium plan. That seems to be the case.\n\nI think I might have been mistaken earlier. So, to clarify, the standard plan includes ads and costs $11.99 a month, while the premium plan is the same but without ads and costs $14.99 a month. Therefore, if someone wants the standard plan without ads, they would have to pay for the premium plan. That makes sense because the premium plan is specifically designed to be ad-free.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the Netflix standard plan with ads is $11.99 a month, and the premium plan without ads is $14.99 a month. Therefore, the standard plan without ads isn't a separate option; instead, the premium plan serves that purpose. So, the answer would be that there isn't a standard plan without ads, but the premium plan is the ad-free version at $14.99 a month.\n\nI think that's the correct way to look at it. So, to answer the question, the standard plan without ads isn't available; instead, the premium plan is the ad-free version at $14.99 a month. Therefore, if someone wants to watch Netflix without ads, they should consider the premium plan.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The Netflix premium plan, which is ad-free, costs $14.99 per month. There isn't a separate standard plan without ads; the premium plan is specifically designed to be ad-free."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I think the standard plan is the most common one, but I'm not sure if it includes ads or not."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-0e3986e73c", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "Who is the current No. 1 on the Billboard Artist 100?", "q_trig": "Who is the current No. 1 on the Billboard Artist 100?", "answer": "BTS", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "he current No. 1 artist on the Artist 100 chart on their website.\n\nAlternatively, you can also check online music platforms like Spotify, Apple Music, or Google Play Music, which often display the current No. 1 artist on their \"Top Artists\" or \"Trending Artists\" sections.\n\nIf you want a more up-to-date answer, I can suggest some popular music news websites or social media platforms that often report on the latest music charts and trends.\n\nLet me know if there's anything else I can help you with!", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "based on my training data but it might be outdated please let me know if you d like me to try ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out who is currently number one on the Billboard Artist 100. I remember that the Billboard Artist 100 is a list of the top 100 selling artists in the world, based on their sales, chart sales, and other metrics. It's updated regularly, so the top spot can change over time.\n\nI think the list is maintained by Billboard magazine, and it's a big deal in the music industry. The current top artist is usually a big name, but I'm not exactly sure who it is off the top of my head. I know that Taylor Swift was number one a few years back, but I think she might have moved up or down since then.\n\nWait, I think it was someone from the 90s or early 2000s. Maybe a rapper? I'm trying to recall... could it be someone like 50 Cent? I think he was up there a few years ago. Or maybe it's a more recent artist like Dua Lipa or someone else.\n\nI should also consider that the list takes into account both physical sales and digital streams. 