{"sid": "z1fresh-4b480e045b", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "When is this year's Black Friday?", "q_trig": "When is this year's Black Friday?", "answer": "November 27", "answer_aliases": ["Nov 27, 2026", "November 27, 2026", "27 Nov 2026", "27 November 2026", "11/27/2026", "11-27-2026", "27/11/2026", "27-11-2026", "2026/11/27"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": " the United States, which falls on the fourth Thursday of November every year. Therefore, the date of Black Friday varies from year to year.\n\nTo give you a more specific answer, here are the dates for Black Friday for the next few years:\n\n* 2023: November 23, 2023\n* 2024: November 29, 2024\n* 2025: November 28, 2025\n\nBlack Friday is traditionally considered the beginning of the holiday shopping season in the United States, and many retailers offer significant discounts and promotions on this day.", "consistency": 0.25, "mode_answer": " here are the dates for black friday for the next few years ", "full_trace": "Alright, the user is asking about this year's Black Friday. I need to provide the date and time. I remember that Black Friday typically happens around the 23rd or 24th of November. I should check the exact date for this year. Let me think, I believe it's November 24, 2023. The time is usually in the evening, maybe around 6 PM in the user's local time. I should mention that the exact time might vary slightly depending on their timezone. Also, I should remind them that Black Friday is a great time for deals, so they should check stores and websites for special offers. I'll make sure to keep the response clear and helpful.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: This year's Black Friday is on **November 24, 2023**, at **6:00 PM** local time. Enjoy shopping with potential discounts!"}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I believe it's November 24, 2023."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-7726d6d7e3", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "When did this year's Passover start?", "q_trig": "When did this year's Passover start?", "answer": "April 1", "answer_aliases": ["Apr 1, 2026", "April 1, 2026", "1 Apr 2026", "1 April 2026", "04/1/2026", "04-1-2026", "1/04/2026", "1-04-2026", "2026/04/1"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "and the sun.\n\nTo find out when Passover starts this year, you can:\n\n1. Check a Jewish calendar or a reliable online source, such as the Hebrew calendar on the website of the Jewish Agency for Israel or the Chabad.org website.\n2. Use a search engine to search for \"Passover 2023\" or \"Passover 2024\" (depending on the current year) to find the exact dates.\n3. Look for a Jewish community or synagogue in your area, which may have a calendar or a website with the dates for Passover.\n\nI hope this helps!", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "if you provide me with the current year i can help you estimate the start date of passover based on the hebrew calendar ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out when Passover started this year. I remember Passover is a Jewish holiday, but I'm not exactly sure about the exact start date. I think it's related to the story of the Exodus from Egypt, where the Israelites were freed from slavery. So, Passover probably starts around the time when they were freed, but I'm not certain about the exact date.\n\nI recall that Passover is celebrated in the spring, so it's likely in March or April. I think it's around the 14th of Nissan, which is the 14th day of the first month in the Hebrew calendar. But I'm not sure if that's the exact start or if it's a specific date in March. Maybe it's around March 14th? That seems familiar.\n\nWait, I think Passover starts on the first day of the month of Nisan, but the 14th of Nisan is when the counting of the Omer begins. So, maybe Passover starts a little before that? Or is it that Passover is celebrated around the time of the 14th of Nisan, but the actual start is the 15th? I'm a bit confused here.\n\nI should also consider that Passover can fall on different dates each year because of the lunar calendar. So, depending on the year, it might be a few days earlier or later in March. For example, in 2023, Passover started on March 26, 2023. But I'm not sure how to determine the exact start date for this year without checking the current year's calendar.\n\nMaybe I can look up the current year's March 14th to see if it's Passover. But I think Passover starts on the 14th of Nisan, which is the 14th day of the first month in the Hebrew calendar. So, I need to find out what date that corresponds to in the Gregorian calendar for this year.\n\nI'm also wondering if Passover starts on the 14th or the 15th. I think it's the 14th, but sometimes it's celebrated on the 15th if the 14th falls on a weekend. So, if March 14th is a Saturday or Sunday, Passover might start on the 15th instead. That makes sense because people don't want to have the holiday start on a weekend.\n\nTo sum up, Passover starts on the 14th of Nisan in the Hebrew calendar, which translates to around March 14th or 15th in the Gregorian calendar. The exact date depends on the year and whether the 14th falls on a weekend. So, for this year, I should check the current year's March 14th to see if it's a weekday or a weekend, and then determine the start date accordingly.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: Passover starts on the 14th of Nisan in the Hebrew calendar, which corresponds to either March 14th or 15th in the Gregorian calendar. The exact start date depends on whether March 14th falls on a weekday or weekend. For this year, Passover began on March 26, 2023."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S4"], "symptom_span": "Passover starts on the 14th of Nisan in the Hebrew calendar, which corresponds to either March 14th or 15th in the Gregorian calendar."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-2220f288ee", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": [], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "Who were the finalists in the US Open women's singles last year?", "q_trig": "Who were the finalists in the US Open women's singles last year?", "answer": "Aryna Sabalenka and Amanda Anisimova", "answer_aliases": ["Amanda Anisimova and Aryna Sabalenka"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "open.org) for the latest news and archives of past tournaments.\n2.  Sports news websites: Websites like ESPN, CNN Sports, or Fox Sports often provide coverage of major sporting events, including the US Open.\n3.  Online sports databases: Websites like Sports-Reference.com or Tennis.com may have archives of past US Open tournaments, including the finalists and winners.\n\nIf you're looking for information on a specific year, please let me know and I can try to help you find the information you need.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "if you re looking for the finalists from a specific year please let me know the year and i can try to help you find the information ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out who were the finalists in the US Open women's singles last year. I'm not exactly sure about the exact names, but I remember that Serena Williams was in the mix. I think she was a finalist, maybe even in the final. I also recall that there was a young player who made a big impression. Was it maybe someone like Bianca Andreescu? I think she was quite talented and had some good matches. There was also a player from Spain, maybe Maria Sakkari? I'm not entirely sure about her name, but she was in the tournament. Another name that comes to mind is Simona Halep, but I'm not certain if she was a finalist. I think she had a tough time in the early rounds. There was also a player from Russia, perhaps Maria Sharapova, but I don't remember her making it far. Maybe it was someone else. I'm trying to remember if