t without access to current information, it's challenging.\n\nI'm starting to realize that without specific knowledge of the current year's Super Bowl broadcast network, it's hard to provide an accurate answer. However, based on my memory, I think the 2023 Super Bowl was on ABC. But I'm not entirely sure, so I might need to verify this.\n\nWait, I think I remember hearing that the 2023 Super Bowl was on ABC. But I'm not certain. Maybe I should try to think about any key moments or ads from that year that were on ABC.\n\nAlternatively, I could think about the NFL's past choices. For example, in 2020, it was NBC, 2021 ABC, 2022 ESPN, and 2023 ABC again. But I'm not sure if that's correct.\n\nI'm also considering that the NFL might have a pattern of rotating through networks every few years. For example, using one network for two years, then switching to another for a couple of years, and so on.\n\nWait, I think in 2022, the Super Bowl was on ESPN, so if that's the case, then 2023 might be back to a different network, perhaps ABC or Cox Digital. But I'm not certain.\n\nI'm starting to realize that without specific information, it's hard to be certain. However, based on my memory, I think the 2023 Super Bowl was on ABC. But I'm not entirely sure, so I might need to check a reliable source or recall any recent information.\n\nWait, I think I remember hearing that the 2023 Super Bowl was on ABC. But I'm not certain. Maybe I should try to think about any key events or ads from that year that were on ABC.\n\nAlternatively, I could think about the NFL's marketing strategies. They might promote the game on certain networks to attract viewers\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The broadcast network for this year's Super Bowl was **ABC**."}, "symptom": ["S3", "S4"], "symptom_span": "I think in 2022, it was ABC. Then in 2023, I believe it was on ABC again."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-ae590796af", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": [], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "What was the highest-grossing film in the United States last year?", "q_trig": "What was the highest-grossing film in the United States last year?", "answer": "Zootopia 2", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "x Office Mojo, IMDB, or Variety for the latest box office data.\n2. Look for recent news articles: Search for news articles from reputable sources like The Hollywood Reporter, The New York Times, or Entertainment Weekly, which often report on the latest box office numbers.\n3. Check the official box office charts: Websites like Box Office Mojo or The Numbers provide up-to-date box office charts and rankings.\n\nIf you're looking for the highest-grossing film of 2021, I can try to help you with that!", "consistency": 0.25, "mode_answer": " ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out what the highest-grossing film in the United States was last year. I'm not exactly sure where to start, but I'll try to break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, I know that movie box offices can be influenced by a lot of factors, like holidays, weekends, and major releases. So, the highest-grossing movie is probably around a time when people are watching a lot of movies, maybe around Christmas or during the summer when more people go to the movies.\n\nI remember hearing about some big movies recently. There was a movie called \"Dune\" that came out a few years ago, but I think it was really popular and made a lot of money. Maybe it's still in the running? Or was it another movie?\n\nWait, I think \"Dune\" was a big hit, but I'm not sure if it was the highest-grossing last year. There was also a movie called \"Tenet\" which came out around the same time as \"Dune.\" I think \"Tenet\" was supposed to be a big one, but I heard some people didn't like it, so maybe it didn't perform as well at the box office.\n\nThen there's \"The Marvels,\" which I think was a superhero movie. It was directed by Ryan Coogler, and I remember it had some controversy because of its casting choices. But did it make a lot of money? I'm not sure.\n\nI also recall that sometimes sequels or remakes can do really well. For example, \"The Matrix 4\" or something like that. But I don't remember the exact numbers.\n\nAnother thought: sometimes the highest-grossing movie isn't a superhero movie. It could be an independent film or a foreign movie. I think there was a big foreign film that was really popular, maybe \"Joker\" was a foreign film? Or was it \"Dune\"?\n\nWait, \"Joker\" was actually a U.S.-produced movie, but it was very popular internationally. I think it made a lot of money, but was it the highest-grossing locally?\n\nI should also consider the box office numbers. I think \"Dune\" made over a billion dollars, but I'm not sure if it was the highest. Maybe \"Tenet\" came close, but maybe it didn't cross the billion mark.\n\nI'm a bit confused because I remember hearing that \"Dune\" was the highest-grossing movie ever, but I'm not certain. Maybe I should think about the release dates. \"Dune\" was released in 2021, so last year would be 2022. Did any other movies come out in 2022 that might have been bigger?\n\nWait, there was a movie called \"The Power of the Dog\" which came out in 2022. I think it was really well-received, but I'm not sure if it made more money than \"Dune.\"\n\nAnother angle: sometimes the highest-grossing movie is a re-release of an older classic. Maybe something like \"The Dark Knight\" or \"Inception\" was re-released and made a lot of money. But I think those were from a few years back, so maybe not the highest of last year.\n\nI'm also thinking about the box office reports. I know that studios like Sony, Universal, and others release a lot of movies each year. Maybe \"Dune\" was a Sony release, and they did well.\n\nWait, I think I've heard that \"Dune\" was the highest-grossing movie ever, making over $1.5 billion. But I'm not 100% sure. Maybe I should check some sources or think about other factors like inflation, but I don't have access to that right now.\n\nSo, putting it all together, I'm leaning towards \"Dune\" being the highest-grossing movie in the U.S. last year, but I'm not entirely certain. It's possible that another movie, maybe a foreign one or a re-release, might have beaten it, but without exact numbers, it's hard to say for sure.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The highest-grossing film in the United States last year was \"Dune,\" which grossed over $1.5 billion."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I think 'Dune' was the highest-grossing movie ever, making over $1.5 billion."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-1680179c1b", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "What Berber year corresponds to the present year?", "q_trig": "What Berber year corresponds to the present year?", "answer": "2976", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "ion, which includes modern-day Morocco, Algeria, Tunisia, Libya, and parts of Mauritania and Mali.\n\nThe Berbers have their own distinct culture, language, and history, but they do not have a calendar system or a way of measuring time that is separate from the Gregorian calendar used internationally.\n\nSo, there is no Berber year that corresponds to the present year. If you meant to ask about the current year in a specific Berber language or culture, please let me know and I'll do my best to help!", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "if you have more context or information about the specific berber calendar you re interested in i d be happy to try and help you determine which year it corresponds to in the gregorian calendar ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out what the current year is in Berber years. I'm not exactly sure what Berber years are, but I think they might be related to the Berber calendar or something like that. Maybe it's similar to the Anno Domini (AD) system we use today, but with a different reference point.\n\nI remember hearing that Berber years are counted from the founding of Carthage, which was around 800 BCE. So, if today is 2023, then the Berber year would be 2023 plus 800, right? That would make it 2823. But wait, I should check if there's a specific name for the current year in Berber. Maybe it's called something like \"Anno Carthaginum\" or \"AC\" for short. I think I've heard that term before.\n\nLet me make sure I'm not mixing this up with other historical calendars. There's also the Julian and Gregorian calendars, but those are for the Christian era. Berber years are more likely a separate system used by the Carthaginians. So, adding 800 years to 2023 should give me the current Berber year. That would be 2823. And the abbreviation is usually \"AC\" for Anno Carthaginum.\n\nI don't think I need to consider BC years here because the Berber calendar starts from 800 BCE, which is the founding of Carthage. So, all years after that would be AD, and before that would be BC. Since we're talking about the present day, which is after 800 BCE, it's definitely in the AD part of the Berber calendar.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the current year in Berber years is 2823 AD, abbreviated as 2823 AC. I think that's the correct answer.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The current year in Berber years is 2823 Anno Carthaginum (AC)."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S4"], "symptom_span": "So, adding 800 years to 2023 should give me the current Berber year. That would be 2823."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-d6163e62c7", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": [], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "When was this year's Ramadan?", "q_trig": "When was this year's Ramadan?", "answer": "Feb 18, 2026 – Mar 19, 2026", "answer_aliases": ["February 18, 2026 – March 19, 2026", "February 18, 2026 – March 19, 2026"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "amadan for any year, along with other Islamic holidays and events.\n2. **Google Search**: Simply type \"Ramadan [current year]\" or \"Ramadan [specific year]\" in Google, and it will show you the dates of Ramadan for that year.\n3. **Local mosques or Islamic centers**: You can also contact your local mosque or Islamic center to ask about the dates of Ramadan for the current year.\n\nPlease let me know if you have a specific year in mind, and I can try to help you find the dates of Ramadan for that year!", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "please let me know if there s anything else i can help you with ", "full_trace": "Alright, the user is asking when this year's Ramadan started. I need to provide the start and end dates. I remember that Ramadan typically begins around the 23rd of the month of Ramadan each year. This year, it started on May 23, 2023, and ended on June 14, 2023. I should make sure to mention the exact dates so the user gets the information they need. Also, I should keep the response clear and straightforward, avoiding any unnecessary details.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: This year's Ramadan began on **May 23, 2023**, and ended on **June 14, 2023**."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S4"], "symptom_span": "Ramadan typically begins around the 23rd of the month of Ramadan each year."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-5bc878895a", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "What date is Thanksgiving this year?", "q_trig": "What date is Thanksgiving this year?", "answer": "November 26", "answer_aliases": ["Nov 26, 2026", "November 26, 2026", "26 Nov 2026", "26 November 2026", "11/26/2026", "11-26-2026", "26/11/2026", "26-11-2026", "2026/11/26"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "now the date of Thanksgiving this year, you can:\n\n1. Check a calendar: Look at a physical or digital calendar to find the fourth Thursday of the current year.\n2. Search online: Type \"Thanksgiving 2023\" (or the current year) in a search engine to find the date.\n3. Ask a voice assistant: If you have a smart speaker or virtual assistant, you can ask it to tell you the date of Thanksgiving this year.\n\nFor example, if you want to know the date of Thanksgiving in 2023, it will be on November 23, 2023.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "please note that thanksgiving is also celebrated in other countries including canada on the second monday in october if you re looking for the canadian thanksgiving date i d be happy to provide that information as well ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out what date Thanksgiving is this year. I remember that Thanksgiving is a holiday celebrated in the United States and some other countries, but I'm not exactly sure about the exact date. I think it's around the fourth Thursday of November, but I'm not 100% certain. Let me try to recall any information I might have come across.\n\nI think in the past, it was always in November, but I'm not sure if it's fixed or if it can change. Maybe it's tied to the Gregorian calendar? I believe the Gregorian calendar is the one we use today, so perhaps that's why the date is fixed. But wait, I also remember that some holidays are tied to religious events, like Christmas, which is December 25th. Thanksgiving, on the other hand, is a secular holiday, so it's based on the agricultural calendar, which might be tied to the phases of the corn harvest.\n\nSo, if Thanksgiving is based on the corn harvest, that would explain why it's around the fourth Thursday of November. I think the reason it's not on a specific date like the 23rd or 24th is because the harvest can occur at different times depending on the weather and the growing season. Therefore, the date can vary a bit each year.\n\nI should also consider how this date is determined. Is there a specific event that marks the beginning of the harvest? Maybe the first Thursday after the first Monday in November? Or is it the fourth Thursday regardless of the year? I think it's the latter. So, to find out the exact date, I might need to look up the current year's calendar.\n\nWait, the user is asking about \"this year,\" which I assume is 2023. So, I should check what the fourth Thursday in November 2023 is. Let me think about the dates in November 2023. November 1st, 2023, was a Sunday. So, the first Thursday would be November 2nd. Then the following Thursdays would be the 9th, 16th, 23rd, and 30th. So, the fourth Thursday would be November 23rd. Therefore, Thanksgiving is on November 23, 2023.\n\nBut wait, I'm not entirely sure if that's correct. Maybe I should double-check the days of the week for November 2023. Let me try to count the days. If November 1st is a Sunday, then November 2nd is Monday, 3rd Tuesday, 4th Wednesday, 5th Thursday. So, the first Thursday is the 5th, not the 2nd. That changes things. So, the first Thursday is November 5th, then the following Thursdays are the 12th, 19th, and 26th. So, the fourth Thursday would be November 26th. Therefore, Thanksgiving is on November 26, 2023.\n\nHmm, I think I made a mistake earlier. I should be careful with the days of the week. Let me recount. November 1, 2023: Sunday. So, November 2 is Monday, 3 Tuesday, 4 Wednesday, 5 Thursday. So, the first Thursday is the 5th. Then, adding seven days each time: 5th, 12th, 19th, 26th. So, the fourth Thursday is the 26th. Therefore, Thanksgiving is on November 26, 2023.\n\nI think that's correct now. So, the answer is November 26, 2023.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: Thanksgiving is on November 26, 2023."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S4"], "symptom_span": "November 23rd. So, the fourth Thursday would be November 23rd."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-36ba51bc81", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "What date is this year's Labor day?", "q_trig": "What date is this year's Labor day?", "answer": "September 7", "answer_aliases": ["Sep 7, 2026", "September 7, 2026", "7 Sep 2026", "7 September 2026", "09/7/2026", "09-7-2026", "7/09/2026", "7-09-2026", "2026/09/7"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "wer:\n\n1. **Check a calendar**: You can check a physical or digital calendar to see the first Monday in September for the current year.\n2. **Search online**: You can search for \"Labor Day 2023\" (or the current year) on a search engine like Google to find the date.\n3. **Ask a voice assistant**: If you have a voice assistant like Siri, Google Assistant, or Alexa, you can ask them to tell you the date of Labor Day this year.\n\nOnce you know the current year, you can easily find the date of Labor Day!", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "assuming you want to know the date for this year 2023 labor day in the united states is on monday september 4th 2023 ", "full_trace": "Alright, so I need to figure out what date Labor Day is this year. I remember that Labor Day is a federal holiday in the United States, but I'm not exactly sure about the specific date. I think it's around the first week of September, but I'm not certain. Maybe I should start by recalling any information I have about it.\n\nI believe that in the past, Labor Day was on the first Monday of September. But I'm not 100% sure if that's still the case or if it's changed over the years. I think it used to be the last working day of the year, but I'm not certain if that's still the tradition. Maybe I should check some sources or think about any patterns I've noticed.\n\nI also remember that sometimes holidays can fall on different days based on the week or the month. For example, if the first of September is a Monday, then Labor Day would be on the 1st. But if the first is a Sunday, then it would be on the 8th. I'm not sure how the date varies from year to year. Maybe I should think about recent years to see if there's a pattern.\n\nWait, I think I heard somewhere that in recent years, Labor Day has been on the 1st of September more often. But I'm not sure if that's accurate. I should try to recall specific years. For example, in 2022, I think Labor Day was on September 5th. That was a Monday. So if that's the case, then in 2023, it might be the next Monday, which would be September 4th. But I'm not certain if that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should think about the Gregorian calendar and how dates shift each year. Since 2023 is not a leap year, the next year, 2024, will have the same calendar as 1988, 1997, etc. But I'm not sure how that affects Labor Day specifically.\n\nI also wonder if there's a mnemonic or a rhyme to remember the date. I recall something like \"The 1st of September, the workers get a day.\" But I'm not sure if that's accurate or if it's just a saying.\n\nAnother approach could be to think about the months. September is the 9th month, so it's towards the end of the year. Labor Day is always in September, but the exact date can vary. I think it's usually the first week, but sometimes it's a bit later depending on the year's start.\n\nI should also consider that in some countries, Labor Day is observed on May 1st, but that's different from the U.S. So I can rule that out. In the U.S., it's definitely around September.\n\nMaybe I can think about the week structure. If the first of September is a Monday, then Labor Day is on the 1st. If it's a Sunday, then it's the 8th. If it's a Tuesday, then it's the 6th, and so on. So the date can shift based on the day of the week the month starts.\n\nI think I should check an online calendar or use a perpetual calendar algorithm to determine the exact date. But since I can't access the internet right now, I need to rely on my memory or any patterns I can recall.\n\nWait, I think in 2023, September 1st was a Sunday. So if that's the case, then Labor Day would be on the 8th of September. But I'm not sure if that's correct. I might be mixing up the days of the week for different years.\n\nAlternatively, if September 1st, 2023, was a Sunday, then adding seven days would make it the 8th. So Labor Day would be on the 8th. But I'm not certain about the actual day of the week for that year.\n\nI think I need to find a way to calculate the day of the week for September 1, 2023. There's a method called Zeller's Congruence that can help with that. Let me try to recall how it works.\n\nZeller's Congruence formula for the Gregorian calendar is:\n\nh = (q + [13(m + 1)/5] + K + [K/4] + [J/4] + 5J) mod 7\n\nWhere:\n- h is the day of the week (0 = Saturday, 1 = Sunday, 2 = Monday, ..., 6 = Friday)\n- q is the day of the month\n- m is the month (3 = March, 4 = April, ..., 14 = February)\n- K is the year of the century (year mod 100)\n- J is the zero-based century (year / 100)\n\nBut since September is the 9th month, m would be 9. However, in Zeller's formula, January and February are counted as months 13 and 14 of the previous year. So for September, m remains 9, and the year remains the same.\n\nLet's apply this to September 1, 2023.\n\nq = 1\nm = 9\nK = 23 (since 2023 mod 100 is 23)\nJ = 20 (since 2023 / 100 is 20.23, so 20)\n\nNow, let's compute each part:\n\nFirst, compute [13(m + 1)/5]:\n13*(9 + 1)/5 = 13*10/5 = 130/5 = 26\n\nNext, compute [K/4]:\n23/4 = 5.75, so the floor is 5\n\nThen, compute [J/4]:\n20/4 = 5, so the floor is 5\n\nNow, plug these into the formula:\n\nh = (1 + 26 + 23 + 5 + 5 + 5*20) mod 7\nh = (1 + 26 + 23 + 5 + 5 + 100) mod 7\nh = (1 + 26 = 27; 27 + 23 = 50; 50 + 5 = 55; 55 + 5 = 60; 60 + 100 = 160) mod 7\n\nNow, 160 divided by 7 is 22 with a remainder. 22*7=154, so 160-154=6. So h=6.\n\nIn Zeller's formula, h=0 is Saturday, 1=Sunday, 2=Monday, ..., 6=Friday. So h=6 corresponds to Friday.\n\nWait, that means September 1, 2023, is a Friday? But I thought it was a Sunday. Hmm, maybe I made a mistake in the calculation.\n\nLet me double-check the formula. Maybe I misapplied it. Alternatively, perhaps I should use a different method, like counting the number of days from a known date.\n\nI know that January 1, 2023, was a Sunday. So if January 1 is a Sunday, then September 1 would be how many days later?\n\nLet's count the days from January 1 to September 1, 2023.\n\nJanuary: 31 days\nFebruary: 28 days (2023 is not a leap year)\nMarch: 31\nApril: 30\nMay: 31\nJune: 30\nJuly: 31\nAugust: 31\nSeptember: 1 (we're counting up to September 1)\n\nWait, no, I think I'm overcomplicating it. From January 1 to September 1 is 8 months. Let's add the days:\n\nJanuary: 31\nFebruary: 28\nMarch: 31\nApril: 30\nMay: 31\nJune: 30\nJuly: 31\nAugust: 31\nSeptember: 1 (but we're stopping at September 1, so we don't include the days in September)\n\nWait, no, actually, if I'm counting from January 1 to September 1, that's 8 months. So the total days would be:\n\n31 (Jan) + 28 (Feb) + 31 (Mar) + 30 (Apr) + 31 (May) + 30 (Jun) + 31 (Jul) + 31 (Aug) = let's add them up.\n\n31 + 28 = 59\n59 + 31 = 90\n90 + 30 = 120\n120 + 31 = 151\n151 + 30 = 181\n181 + 31 = 212\n212 + 31 = 243\n\nSo from January 1 to September 1 is 243 days.\n\nNow, since January 1, 2023, was a Sunday, we can find out what day September 1 is by calculating 243 mod 7.\n\n243 divided by 7 is 34 weeks and 5 days (since 34*7=238, 243-238=\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: September 4, 2023."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I think in 2023, September 1st was a Sunday."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-cb6c88284f", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": [], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "What was the host stadium of this year's Super Bowl?", "q_trig": "What was the host stadium of this year's Super Bowl?", "answer": "Levi's Stadium", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "um of this year's Super Bowl.\n\nBut I can suggest some possible ways for you to find the answer:\n\n1. Check the official NFL website or social media channels for the latest information on the Super Bowl.\n2. Look for news articles or sports websites that cover the NFL and the Super Bowl.\n3. Check the official website of the NFL team that hosted the Super Bowl.\n\nIf you provide me with the year of the Super Bowl you're interested in, I can try to help you find the host stadium for that specific year.", "consistency": 0.25, "mode_answer": " ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out what the host stadium was for this year's Super Bowl. Hmm, I'm not exactly sure about the exact details, but I remember that the Super Bowl is the biggest American football game, right? It's between the winner of the NFL championship and another team, usually the Super Bowl LVI or something like that.\n\nI think the Super Bowl is held in a different city each year, but I'm not certain which one it was this time. I believe it's usually in a major city with a big stadium. Maybe somewhere like New York, but I'm not sure. Wait, I think it was in a city that's a big business convention center because I remember something about the NFL choosing a city that had a convention center as the host.\n\nOh, right! I think it was in Houston, Texas. There's a place called NRG Stadium there, which I've heard about because it's a really big stadium. I think they built it specifically for the Super Bowl. So, maybe this year's Super Bowl was held at NRG Stadium in Houston.\n\nBut wait, I'm not 100% sure. I should double-check. I know that sometimes the Super Bowl moves around, and the host city changes. I think in recent years, it's been in different places like Atlanta, then maybe in a few others, but I'm pretty sure Houston was the host this year. Yeah, I'm going to go with that. So, the host stadium for this year's Super Bowl was NRG Stadium in Houston, Texas.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The host stadium for this year's Super Bowl was NRG Stadium in Houston, Texas."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I think it was in a city that's a big business convention center"}
{"sid": "z1fresh-460592021d", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "What date was the State of the Union Address delivered in the previous calendar year?", "q_trig": "What date was the State of the Union Address delivered in the previous calendar year?", "answer": "Mar 4, 2025", "answer_aliases": ["March 4, 2025", "4 Mar 2025", "4 March 2025", "03/4/2025", "03-4-2025", "4/03/2025", "4-03-2025", "2025/03/4", "2025-03-4"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "r's State of the Union Address.\n2. Search online: You can try searching for \"State of the Union Address [previous year]\" or \"State of the Union Address [previous year's date]\" to find relevant results.\n3. Check news archives: Websites like CNN, Fox News, or other news outlets often archive their coverage of the State of the Union Address, including the date and time it was delivered.\n\nIf you provide me with the previous year, I can try to help you find the date of the State of the Union Address.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "if you re looking for the date of a specific state of the union address i can try to help you with that please let me know which year you re interested in and i ll do my best to provide you with the date of the address ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out what date the previous State of the Union Address was delivered. Hmm, I'm not exactly sure, but I think the State of the Union is an annual address given by the President of the United States. I remember hearing about it in school or maybe on the news. Let me think about how these addresses are timed.\n\nI know that the current President is Joe Biden, and I think he gave his State of the Union address in February 2022. So if the current year is 2023, the previous one would be 2022. But wait, the user is asking for the date of the previous calendar year's address. So if the current year is 2023, the previous year is 2022, and the address was in February 2022. But I should make sure that's correct.\n\nI also recall that the State of the Union addresses are typically delivered in January or February. They're usually in the first part of the year because that's when the new administration starts. So if Biden was in office in 2022, his address would have been in February 2022. That makes sense because the transition happens in January, and the address is a key speech for the new year.\n\nWait, but sometimes the exact date can vary. I think it's usually around the first of February. Let me try to remember any specific details. I think it was February 1, 2022. That seems right because I remember watching it on TV that day. So the previous year's address would be the one before that, but since the current year is 2023, the previous one is 2022, and the address was on February 1, 2022.\n\nI should also consider if there's any chance it's in January. But no, the State of the Union is usually delivered in February to coincide with the start of the new year. So I'm pretty confident it's February 1, 2022. Therefore, the date of the previous State of the Union Address would be February 1, 2022.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The previous State of the Union Address was delivered on February 1, 2022."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S4"], "symptom_span": "The previous State of the Union Address was delivered on February 1, 2022."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-54bd8f8d3b", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": [], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fast-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "What won the latest Academy Award for Best Animated Feature?", "q_trig": "What won the latest Academy Award for Best Animated Feature?", "answer": "KPop Demon Hunters", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": " find the answer:\n\n1. Check the official Academy Awards website (oscars.org) for the latest winners.\n2. Look up entertainment news websites or online publications, such as Variety, The Hollywood Reporter, or Entertainment Weekly, which often report on the latest awards news.\n3. Search for \"latest Academy Award for Best Animated Feature\" on a search engine like Google.\n\nIf you're looking for the winner from a specific year, feel free to let me know, and I'll do my best to provide the information!", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "please let me know if i can help you with anything else ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out what won the latest Academy Award for Best Animated Feature. I remember that the Oscars are happening soon, but I'm not exactly sure about the details. Let me think about this step by step.\n\nFirst, I know that the Oscars are held in March, so the latest one would be the 94th Academy Awards. I'm trying to recall which movie was up for that award. I think it's one of the recent animated films, maybe from 2021 or 2022. I remember some big names in animation, like Disney, Pixar, and BAFTA. \n\nI think the movie was called \"The Power of the Snake.\" Wait, no, that doesn't sound right. Maybe it's \"The Power of the Snake\" but I'm not certain. Alternatively, I might be confusing it with another animated film. Let me try to recall the names of some recent animated movies. There's \"Inside Out,\" \"Zootopia,\" \"Coco,\" \"The Incredibles,\" \"The Good guys,\" \"Inside Man,\" \"The Pink Panther,\" \"The Iron Giant,\" \"The Princess and t