ng a favorite among many readers. Alternatively, maybe it was \"The Three-Body Problem\" by Liu Cixin, but I think that's more of a China Miéville collaboration. Wait, no, Liu Cixin won a Hugo for that, but I'm not sure about the Nebula.\n\nAnother possibility is \"The Art of Fielding\" by Chad Harbach and Daniel Abraham. I think that was a strong contender, but I'm not certain if it won the Nebula. Maybe \"The Three-Body Problem\" was the Nebula winner? Or perhaps \"The Name of the Wind\" won the Hugo, and the Nebula was something else.\n\nI should also consider that sometimes the Nebula and Hugo awards are given in the same year, but sometimes they're different. I think in 2022, \"The Name of the Wind\" won the Hugo, so maybe the Nebula was given to another book. I'm trying to recall if it was \"The Three-Body Problem\" or another book. Wait, I think \"The Three-Body Problem\" was the Hugo winner, but I'm not sure about the Nebula.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"The Left Hand of Darkness\" was the Nebula winner. I think that's a solid possibility. I should also consider that sometimes the Nebula might not be as widely publicized as the Hugo, so maybe it's less known to the general public. \n\nI'm a bit stuck here. I think I should try to recall any recent announcements or news about Nebula awards. I believe in recent years, the Nebula has been given to books like \"The Three-Body Problem,\" but I'm not certain. Alternatively, maybe \"The Art of Fielding\" was the winner. I'm not entirely sure, but I think the Nebula for Best Novel in 2023 was awarded to \"The Left Hand of Darkness\" byJD Sneed. \n\nWait, I'm confusing myself. I think I need to verify this. I'll try to remember any notable mentions. I think \"The Left Hand of Darkness\" is a strong contender, but I'm not sure if it won. Maybe it was \"The Three-Body Problem\" that won the Nebula. Alternatively, perhaps \"The Name of the Wind\" won the Nebula, but I thought that was the Hugo. \n\nI'm going to go with \"T