 the timing, the next Academy Awards are usually a few months away. If he's been dealing with personal issues, he might have had to step back to take care of them before resuming public appearances.\n\nSo, putting it all together, it seems like there are several reasons why Will Smith might not attend. Personal issues, focusing on projects, legal matters, or stepping back from the spotlight. Without specific information, it's hard to be certain, but these are the plausible reasons.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: Will Smith may not attend the next Academy Awards show due to personal issues, legal matters, or stepping back from public appearances to focus on personal or family matters."}, "symptom": ["S3", "S1"], "symptom_span": "Will Smith may not attend the next Academy Awards show due to personal issues, legal matters, or stepping back from public appearances to focus on personal or family matters."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-cc966bded0", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": [], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "How many people from Princeton University have won the Fields Medal? ", "q_trig": "How many people from Princeton University have won the Fields Medal? ", "answer": "9", "answer_aliases": ["nine"], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "I am unable to verify how many people from Princeton University have won the Fields Medal.", "consistency": 0.25, "mode_answer": "i am unable to verify how many people from princeton university have won the fields medal ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many people from Princeton University have won the Fields Medal. I'm not too familiar with all the details, but I know the Fields Medal is a big deal in mathematics, kind of like the Nobel Prize but for mathematicians. It's awarded every four years to up to four people under 40. \n\nFirst, I should probably start by recalling any famous mathematicians from Princeton. I know that Princeton has a strong mathematics department, especially because of its connection to the Institute for Advanced Study. Some names that come to mind are John Milnor, who I think was a Putnam Fellow a lot, and maybe some others like Andrew Wiles. Wait, Andrew Wiles is from Cambridge, though, right? He went to Oxford for his doctorate but is now at Princeton. So maybe he counts as a Princetonian.\n\nI remember that John Milnor won the Fields Medal in 1962. That's one. Then there's William Thurston, who I believe was from New York City and went to MIT, but I'm not sure if he was at Princeton. Maybe he was a visitor or something. I'm not certain about his affiliation with Princeton.\n\nAnother name that pops up is Daniel Quillen. I think he was at MIT, but I'm not sure if he was affiliated with Princeton. He won the Fields Medal in 1978, so that's another one if he was indeed from Princeton.\n\nThen there's Shing-Tung Yau. I'm pretty sure he went to Harvard, but I'm not sure if he was ever at Princeton. He won the Fields Medal in 1982, so if he was at Princeton, that would add another to the count.\n\nWait, I also remember that John Nash was a Princeton alumnus. He was awarded the Nobel Prize, but did he get the Fields Medal too? I think he was too young when the Fields Medal was first awarded, so maybe he didn't win it. He won the Nobel instead. So that's not a Fields Medal winner.\n\nThere's also Simon Donaldson, who I think was at Oxford but might have been at the Institute for Advanced Study, which is part of Princeton's campus. So maybe he's considered a Princetonian for this purpose. He won the Fields Medal in 1986.\n\nThen there's Maxim Kontsevich. I believe he was at the Max Planck Institute in Germany, but I'm not sure if he was ever at Princeton. He won the Fields Medal in 1998, so if he was at Princeton, that would be another one.\n\nGrigori Perelman is another name that comes up, but I don't think he was at Princeton. He's from Russia and worked at the Steklov Institute in St. Petersburg. He famously declined the Fields Medal in 2006, so that's not relevant here.\n\nSo, putting this together, the ones I can confirm are John Milnor (1962), Simon Donaldson (1986), and maybe Daniel Quillen (1978) if he was at Princeton. I'm not sure about William Thurston and Shing-Tung Yau's affiliations. Also, Shing-Tung Yau was at Harvard, so probably not. \n\nWait, I should check if Daniel Quillen was indeed at Princeton. I think he was a visiting professor or something, but I'm not certain. If he was, that would make him the third. Otherwise, it's just Milnor and Donaldson. \n\nSo, tentatively, I think there are at least two people from Princeton who have won the Fields Medal: John Milnor and Simon Donaldson. If Daniel Quillen was indeed a Princetonian, that would make three. But I'm not entirely sure about his affiliation. \n\nI should also consider if there are any others. Maybe someone like John Tate, but I don't think he won the Fields Medal. He won the Abel Prize, which is different. \n\nSo, to sum up, I believe the number is two or three, depending on Daniel Quillen's status. But I'm not 100% certain about his affiliation with Princeton. Maybe I should look up a list or something, but since I can't do that right now, I'll go with the two I'm pretty sure about: John Milnor and Simon Donaldson.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: Two people from Princeton University have won the Fields Medal. These individuals are John Milnor, who received the award in 1962, and Simon Donaldson, who was awarded in 1986. Daniel Quillen, while associated with Princeton, is not definitively confirmed as a Fields Medal winner."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I'm not too familiar with all the details, but I know the Fields Medal is a big deal in mathematics, kind of like the Nobel Prize but for mathematicians."}
{"sid": "z1fresh-b7e56f6148", "stressor": "Z1", "secondary_labels": ["Z6"], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-slow-changing", "intensity": 0.0, "q_clean": "Who is the star of the international series \"It's me or the dog\" married to?", "q_trig": "Who is the star of the international series \"It's me or the dog\" married to?", "answer": "Van Zeiler", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "The star of the international series \"It's Me or the Dog\" is Victoria Stilwell.", "consistency": 0.125, "mode_answer": "i am not familiar with a series called it s me or the dog ", "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out who the star of the international series \"It's Me or the Dog\" is married to. I'm not too familiar with the show, but I know it's a reality TV show. Let me start by recalling what I know about the show.\n\n\"**It's Me or the Dog**\" is a reality TV show, I think it's similar to other shows like \"Big Brother\" or \"The Great British Baking Competition,\" but I'm not entirely sure. I believe it's about a group of people living together in a house, and they have to deal with various challenges and conflicts. Maybe it's about a dog as well, hence the name.\n\nI remember that the show features a group of strangers who end up living together, and they have to manage their relationships, work on their relationships, and deal with any issues that arise. The show is probably centered around a dog, so maybe the main character is the dog, and the others are people who interact with it.\n\nNow, the user is asking about the star of the show, who is the main character, and who they are married to. So, I need to find out who the main character is and then determine their spouse.\n\nI think the main character is named Tia, but I'm not 100% sure. Maybe it's someone else. Let me try to recall or think about other similar shows. I know that in some shows, the main character is often the one who stands out, maybe the only human in the